window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-14"]=[{"id":"q2-233","q":"2:233","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:233 · Nursing, mutual consultation and spiritual nurture","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 261–262","cairo":"I:261–262","source_page_first":261,"source_page_last":262,"ar":"وَالْوالِداتُ يُرْضِعْنَ أَوْلادَهُنَّ حَوْلَيْنِ كامِلَيْنِ لِمَنْ أَرادَ أَنْ يُتِمَّ الرَّضاعَةَ وَعَلَى الْمَوْلُودِ لَهُ رِزْقُهُنَّ وَكِسْوَتُهُنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ لا تُكَلَّفُ نَفْسٌ إِلاَّ وُسْعَها لا تُضَارَّ والِدَةٌ بِوَلَدِها وَلا مَوْلُودٌ لَهُ بِوَلَدِهِ وَعَلَى الْوارِثِ مِثْلُ ذلِكَ فَإِنْ أَرادا فِصالاً عَنْ تَراضٍ مِنْهُما وَتَشاوُرٍ فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْهِما وَإِنْ أَرَدْتُمْ أَنْ تَسْتَرْضِعُوا أَوْلادَكُمْ فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ إِذا سَلَّمْتُمْ ما آتَيْتُمْ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ بَصِيرٌ (233)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ويجب على الوالدات أن يُرْضِعْنَ أَوْلادَهُنَّ حَوْلَيْنِ كامِلَيْنِ إذا كُنَّ في العصمة، ولا شرف لهن لجرى العُرف بذلك، أو مطلقاتٍ، ولم يقبل الولد غيرهنَّ، هذا لِمَنْ أَرادَ أَنْ يُتِمَّ الرَّضاعَةَ، فإن اتفقا على فطامه قبلهما، جاز، كما يأتي. ويجب عَلَى الْمَوْلُودِ لَهُ وهو الأب، رزق أمهات أولاده، وَكِسْوَتُهُنَّ إذ هو الذي يُنسب المولود له، وذلك بِالْمَعْرُوفِ، لا يكلف الله نفسا إلا ما في وُسْعها وتُطيقه، فلا تُضَارَّ والِدَةٌ بِوَلَدِها، بحيث ترضعه وهي مريضة، أو انقطع لبنها. بل يجب على الأب أن يستأجر من يرضعه، ولا يضار مَوْلُودٌ لَهُ بِوَلَدِهِ، بحيث يكلف من الإنفاق والكسوة فوق جهده. فإن مات الأب وترك مالاً- فعلى الْوارِثِ الكبير مِثْلُ ذلِكَ من الكسوة والإنفاق، يُجريها من مال الأب، ويحسبها من حق الصبي، فإن لم يكن للأب مال- فعلى جماعة المسلمين.\nفَإِنْ أَرادا أي: الأب والمرضعة، فِصالًا أي: فطاماً للصبي قبل تمام الحولين، عَنْ تَراضٍ مِنْهُما وَتَشاوُرٍ بينهما، فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْهِما، إن لم يخف على الولد ضعف. وَإِنْ أَرَدْتُمْ، أيها الأزواج، أَنْ تَسْتَرْضِعُوا أَوْلادَكُمْ عند غير الأم، برضاها، فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ في ذلك إِذا سَلَّمْتُمْ أي: أعطيتم للمراضع، ما آتَيْتُمْ أي: ما أردتم إيتاءه من الأجرة بِالْمَعْرُوفِ من غير مَطْلٍ ولا تقتير. والشرط إنما هو\nعلى وجه الكمال والإحسان، وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ فيما كُلفتم به من الحقوق، وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ لا يخفى عليه شيء من أموركم فإنه بِما تَعْمَلُونَ بَصِيرٌ.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن تربية الولاية في قلب المريد، على نمط تربية الطفل الصغير، تنبت في قلب المريد وقت عقد الصحبة بينهما، ثم لا تزال تنمو، أو الشيخ يرضعه بلبن الإمداد حتى يتم أوان رضاعه، ولذلك قالوا: الثدي الميتة لا ترضع. هـ. يشيرون إلى أن الشيخ الميت لا يُربى، فلا يزال الشيخ يُربى الروح، ويمدها حتى تدخل بلد الإحسان، وتشتعل فكرتها. وهذا تمام الحولين في حقها، وهو أوان كمال الحقيقة والشريعة لمن أراد إتمامها، فتأكل الروح حينئذٍ من كل شيء، وتشرب من كل شيء، وتستمد من الأشياء كلها، ثم لا يزال يحاذيها بهمته حتى تَرشُد، فيطلق لها التصرف، فتصلح لتربية غيرها.\nوعلى الشيخ رزقُ المريدين من قوت القلوب وكسوتهم، تقيهم من إصابة الذنوب والعيوب، إلا ما سبق به القضاءُ في علم الغيوب، فليس في طَوْق أحدٍ دفعُه، لا تُكلف نفسٌ إلا وسعها، فإذا مات الشيخ، ووصَّى بمن يرث مقامه، فعلى الوارث مثلُ ذلك، فإن أراد المريد انفصالاً عن الشيخ، وتعمير بلد، أو تذكير عباد الله، عن تراض منهما وتشاور من الشيخ، فلا جناح عليهما، وإن أردتم، أيها الشيوخ، أن تسترضعوا أولادكم بإرسال منْ يُذكِّرُهم، ويمدهم، نائباً عنكم، فلا جناح عليكم إذا سلمتم لهم من الإمداد ما يمدهم به، واتقوا الله في شأن المريدين، في جبر كسرهم، وقبول عذرهم، واعلموا أن الله بما تعملون بصير.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى عدّة الوفاة، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَالْوالِداتُ يُرْضِعْنَ أَوْلادَهُنَّ حَوْلَيْنِ كامِلَيْنِ لِمَنْ أَرادَ أَنْ يُتِمَّ الرَّضاعَةَ وَعَلَى الْمَوْلُودِ لَهُ رِزْقُهُنَّ وَكِسْوَتُهُنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ لا تُكَلَّفُ نَفْسٌ إِلاَّ وُسْعَها لا تُضَارَّ والِدَةٌ بِوَلَدِها وَلا مَوْلُودٌ لَهُ بِوَلَدِهِ وَعَلَى الْوارِثِ مِثْلُ ذلِكَ فَإِنْ أَرادا فِصالاً عَنْ تَراضٍ مِنْهُما وَتَشاوُرٍ فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْهِما وَإِنْ أَرَدْتُمْ أَنْ تَسْتَرْضِعُوا أَوْلادَكُمْ فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ إِذا سَلَّمْتُمْ ما آتَيْتُمْ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ بَصِيرٌ (233)","quran_en":"233. Mothers shall nurse their children for two complete years, for whoever desires to complete the nursing. The father shall provide their food and clothing in a right manner. No soul is charged beyond its capacity. No mother shall be harmed on account of her child, nor any father on account of his child. The heir bears a like duty. If the two desire weaning by mutual agreement and consultation, there is no blame upon them. If you wish to have your children nursed by another, there is no blame upon you, provided you hand over what you have agreed to give in a right manner. Be mindful of God, and know that God sees what you do.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Nursing, maintenance and the child’s welfare","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: mothers must nurse their children for two complete years when they remain in marriage and are not women of high social standing, since this is the customary practice; or when they are divorced and the child will accept no other nurse. This is for whoever desires to complete the nursing. If the two agree to wean the child earlier, it is permissible, as will follow.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-179\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B173]<\/a><\/p><p>The father—the one to whom the child is ascribed—must provide the food and clothing of his children’s mothers, in a right manner. God charges no soul beyond what lies within its capacity and what it can bear. A mother is therefore not to be harmed on account of her child, by having to nurse while ill or when her milk has ceased; the father must instead hire someone to nurse the child. Nor is a father to be harmed on account of his child by being charged with maintenance and clothing beyond his means. If the father dies leaving property, the adult heir bears a like duty of clothing and maintenance, paying it out of the father’s property and reckoning it against the child’s share. If the father leaves no property, the duty falls upon the Muslim community.<\/p><p>If the father and nursing woman desire to wean the child before the completion of two years, by mutual agreement and consultation, there is no blame upon them, provided that no weakening of the child is feared. If you, husbands, wish to have your children nursed by someone other than their mother, with her consent, there is no blame upon you, provided you give the nurses the payment you intend to give in a right manner, without delay or stinting. This condition is stated by way of<\/p><p>perfection and good conduct. Be mindful of God concerning the rights with which you have been charged, and know that nothing in your affairs is hidden from God, for He sees what you do.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: mothers must nurse their children for two complete years when they remain in marriage and are not women of high social standing, since this is the customary practice; or when they are divorced and the child will accept no other nurse. This is for whoever desires to complete the nursing. If the two agree to wean the child earlier, it is permissible, as will follow. [B173] The father—the one to whom the child is ascribed—must provide the food and clothing of his children’s mothers, in a right manner. God charges no soul beyond what lies within its capacity and what it can bear. A mother is therefore not to be harmed on account of her child, by having to nurse while ill or when her milk has ceased; the father must instead hire someone to nurse the child. Nor is a father to be harmed on account of his child by being charged with maintenance and clothing beyond his means. If the father dies leaving property, the adult heir bears a like duty of clothing and maintenance, paying it out of the father’s property and reckoning it against the child’s share. If the father leaves no property, the duty falls upon the Muslim community. If the father and nursing woman desire to wean the child before the completion of two years, by mutual agreement and consultation, there is no blame upon them, provided that no weakening of the child is feared. If you, husbands, wish to have your children nursed by someone other than their mother, with her consent, there is no blame upon you, provided you give the nurses the payment you intend to give in a right manner, without delay or stinting. This condition is stated by way of perfection and good conduct. Be mindful of God concerning the rights with which you have been charged, and know that nothing in your affairs is hidden from God, for He sees what you do."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The growth of sainthood and the responsibility of the guide","html":"<p>Know that the nurture of sainthood (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الولاية<\/bdi>) within the disciple’s heart follows the pattern of nurturing a little child. It takes root in his heart when the bond of companionship is established between him and the shaykh. It then continues to grow, while the shaykh nurses him with the milk of spiritual support (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإمداد<\/bdi>), until his period of nursing is complete. Hence their saying: “A dead breast does not nurse.” They indicate by this that a deceased shaykh does not provide this formative nurture.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-180\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B174]<\/a><\/p><p>The shaykh continues to nurture and sustain the spirit until it enters the country of spiritual excellence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإحسان<\/bdi>) and its contemplative thought is kindled. This is the completion of its two years: the time when Reality and Sacred Law are brought to perfection, for whoever desires their completion. The spirit then eats of everything, drinks of everything, and draws nourishment from all things. The shaykh continues to attend it with his spiritual aspiration (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الهمة<\/bdi>) until it reaches maturity. He then releases it to act, and it becomes fit to nurture another.<\/p><p>The shaykh is responsible for feeding the disciples with the sustenance of hearts and clothing them in what protects them from sins and faults—except what the decree has already determined in the knowledge of the unseen, for no one has the power to repel that. “No soul is charged beyond its capacity.” If the shaykh dies and designates someone to inherit his station, the heir bears a like duty.<\/p><p>If a disciple wishes to separate from the shaykh, establish a centre in another place or remind God’s servants, by mutual agreement and consultation with the shaykh, there is no blame upon them. If you, shaykhs, wish to have your children nursed by sending someone to remind and sustain them as your deputy, there is no blame upon you, provided you impart to him the spiritual support through which he can sustain them. Be mindful of God in the affairs of disciples: mend their brokenness and accept their excuses. Know that God sees what you do.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Know that the nurture of sainthood ( الولاية ) within the disciple’s heart follows the pattern of nurturing a little child. It takes root in his heart when the bond of companionship is established between him and the shaykh. It then continues to grow, while the shaykh nurses him with the milk of spiritual support ( الإمداد ), until his period of nursing is complete. Hence their saying: “A dead breast does not nurse.” They indicate by this that a deceased shaykh does not provide this formative nurture. [B174] The shaykh continues to nurture and sustain the spirit until it enters the country of spiritual excellence ( الإحسان ) and its contemplative thought is kindled. This is the completion of its two years: the time when Reality and Sacred Law are brought to perfection, for whoever desires their completion. The spirit then eats of everything, drinks of everything, and draws nourishment from all things. The shaykh continues to attend it with his spiritual aspiration ( الهمة ) until it reaches maturity. He then releases it to act, and it becomes fit to nurture another. The shaykh is responsible for feeding the disciples with the sustenance of hearts and clothing them in what protects them from sins and faults—except what the decree has already determined in the knowledge of the unseen, for no one has the power to repel that. “No soul is charged beyond its capacity.” If the shaykh dies and designates someone to inherit his station, the heir bears a like duty. If a disciple wishes to separate from the shaykh, establish a centre in another place or remind God’s servants, by mutual agreement and consultation with the shaykh, there is no blame upon them. If you, shaykhs, wish to have your children nursed by sending someone to remind and sustain them as your deputy, there is no blame upon you, provided you impart to him the spiritual support through which he can sustain them. Be mindful of God in the affairs of disciples: mend their brokenness and accept their excuses. Know that God sees what you do."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then mentions the waiting period following a husband’s death.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then mentions the waiting period following a husband’s death."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3946,"arabic_characters":3079},{"id":"q2-234-235","q":"2:234–235","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:234–235 · Widowhood, guarded proposals and the time of spiritual maturity","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 262–264","cairo":"I:262–264","source_page_first":262,"source_page_last":264,"ar":"وَالَّذِينَ يُتَوَفَّوْنَ مِنْكُمْ وَيَذَرُونَ أَزْواجاً يَتَرَبَّصْنَ بِأَنْفُسِهِنَّ أَرْبَعَةَ أَشْهُرٍ وَعَشْراً فَإِذا بَلَغْنَ أَجَلَهُنَّ فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ فِيما فَعَلْنَ فِي أَنْفُسِهِنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ خَبِيرٌ (234) وَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ فِيما عَرَّضْتُمْ بِهِ مِنْ خِطْبَةِ النِّساءِ أَوْ أَكْنَنْتُمْ فِي أَنْفُسِكُمْ عَلِمَ اللَّهُ أَنَّكُمْ سَتَذْكُرُونَهُنَّ وَلكِنْ لا تُواعِدُوهُنَّ سِرًّا إِلاَّ أَنْ تَقُولُوا قَوْلاً مَعْرُوفاً وَلا تَعْزِمُوا عُقْدَةَ النِّكاحِ حَتَّى يَبْلُغَ الْكِتابُ أَجَلَهُ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ يَعْلَمُ مَا فِي أَنْفُسِكُمْ فَاحْذَرُوهُ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ حَلِيمٌ (235)\nقلت: والَّذِينَ يُتَوَفَّوْنَ: مبتدأ، ويَتَرَبَّصْنَ: خبر، ولا بد من الحذف ليصح الإخبار، إما من الصدر أو من العَجزُ، أي: وأزواج الذين يتوفون، أو الذين يتوفون أزواجهن يتربصن.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَالَّذِينَ يموتون منكم، أيها المؤمنون، ويتركون أَزْواجاً، فلا يتزوجن حتى يَتَرَبَّصْنَ أي: يمكثن بِأَنْفُسِهِنَّ أَرْبَعَةَ أَشْهُرٍ وعشرة أيام لأن الجنين يتحرك لثلاثة أشهر إن كان ذكراً، ولأربعة إن كان أنثى في الغالب(1) ، وزيد عشرةً، استظهاراً، هذا في غير الحامل، أما الحامل، فعدتها وضع حملها. فَإِذا بَلَغْنَ أَجَلَهُنَّ أي: انقضت عدتهن، فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ أيها الأولياء فِيما فَعَلْنَ فِي أَنْفُسِهِنَّ من التزين والتعرض للنكاح أو التزوج، بِالْمَعْرُوفِ، بحيث لا ينكره الشرع من تزين ونكاح، وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ خَبِيرٌ فيجازيكم على ما فعلتم.\nوَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ أيها الخُطَّاب فِيما عَرَّضْتُمْ بِهِ للمعتدات مِنْ خِطْبَةِ النِّساءِ كقول الرجل:\nإني لراغب في صحبتكم، وإني أريد أن أتزوج في هذه الساعة. وإنك لنافقة(2)، أو لا يصلح لك أن تبقى بلا زوج، ونحو هذا، أَوْ أَكْنَنْتُمْ أي: أضمرتم فِي أَنْفُسِكُمْ في زمن العدة من أمر التزوج دون تصريح، عَلِمَ اللَّهُ أَنَّكُمْ ستذكرون النساء المعتدات، وتتكلمون في نكاحهن، حرصاً وتمنياً، فعرّضُوا بذلك، وَلكِنْ لا تُواعِدُوهُنَّ سِرًّا أي: في الخلوة، أو لا تواعدوهن نكاحاً أو جماعاً، إِلَّا أَنْ تَقُولُوا قَوْلًا مَعْرُوفاً وهو التعريض بالألفاظ المتقدمة.\nولا تقطعوا عُقْدَةَ النِّكاحِ، وتعزموا على فعله، حَتَّى يَبْلُغَ كتاب المعتدة أَجَلَهُ، وتنقضى العدة، وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ يَعْلَمُ ما فِي أَنْفُسِكُمْ من الرغبة والحرص، فَاحْذَرُوهُ فإنَّ الحرص على الشيء، والرغبة فيه، قبل أوانه، ربما يعاقب صاحبه بحرمانه، وما قُدِّر لكَ لا يكون لغيرك، وما كان لغيرك لا يكون لك، ولو فعلت ما فعلت، وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ لِمَا استعجلتم فإن الإنسان خُلق عجولاً، حَلِيمٌ فلا يعاجلكم ولا يفضح سرائركم.\nالإشارة: إذا ماتت النفس عن الهوى، وتركت حظوظاً وشهوات، فلا ينبغي أن يردها إلى ذلك حتى تتربص مدة، ليظهر عليها آثارُ الزهد من السكون إلى الله، والتأنس بمشاهدة الله حتى تغيب عما سواه. فإذا بلغت هذا الوصف فلا جناح على المريد أن يسعفها فيما تفعل بالمعروف، من غير سَرَفٍ ولا ميل إلى هوى، لأن فعلها حينئذٍ بالله، ومن الله، وإلى الله، وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ خَبِيرٌ لا يخفى عليه شيء من أمرها، ولا جناح عليكم، أيها المريدون، إن تزكت نفوسكم، وطهرت من الأغيار قلوبكم، فيما عرضتم به من خطبة أبكار الحقائق وثيبات العلوم، أو أكننتم في أنفسكم من المعارف والمفهوم، علم الله أنكم ستذكرون ذلك باللسان قبل أن يصل الذوق إلى الجَنَان، فلا تصرحوا بعلوم الحقائق مع كل الخلائق فإن ذلك من فعل الزنادق، إلا أن تقولوا قولاً معروفاً، إشارة أو تلويحاً، فعلمنا كله إشارة، فإذا صار عبارة خفي.\nولا تطلبوا علم الحقائق قبل بلوغ أجله، وهو موت النفوس، والزهد في الفلوس، وكمال التربية، وتمام التصفية، وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ يَعْلَمُ ما فِي أَنْفُسِكُمْ من الشرة إليها قبل أوانها، فَاحْذَرُوهُ أن يعاقبكم بحرمانها، وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ حَلِيمٌ لا يعاجلكم بحرمان قصدكم، إن صح مقصدكم، والله تعالى أعلم، وبالله التوفيق.\nثم ذكر الحقّ جلّ جلاله حكم الطلاق قبل المسيس، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَالَّذِينَ يُتَوَفَّوْنَ مِنْكُمْ وَيَذَرُونَ أَزْواجاً يَتَرَبَّصْنَ بِأَنْفُسِهِنَّ أَرْبَعَةَ أَشْهُرٍ وَعَشْراً فَإِذا بَلَغْنَ أَجَلَهُنَّ فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ فِيما فَعَلْنَ فِي أَنْفُسِهِنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ خَبِيرٌ (234) وَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ فِيما عَرَّضْتُمْ بِهِ مِنْ خِطْبَةِ النِّساءِ أَوْ أَكْنَنْتُمْ فِي أَنْفُسِكُمْ عَلِمَ اللَّهُ أَنَّكُمْ سَتَذْكُرُونَهُنَّ وَلكِنْ لا تُواعِدُوهُنَّ سِرًّا إِلاَّ أَنْ تَقُولُوا قَوْلاً مَعْرُوفاً وَلا تَعْزِمُوا عُقْدَةَ النِّكاحِ حَتَّى يَبْلُغَ الْكِتابُ أَجَلَهُ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ يَعْلَمُ مَا فِي أَنْفُسِكُمْ فَاحْذَرُوهُ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ حَلِيمٌ (235)","quran_en":"234. Those of you who die and leave wives behind: their wives shall wait by themselves for four months and ten days. When they reach the end of their term, there is no blame upon you concerning what they do with themselves in a right manner. God is aware of what you do. 235. There is no blame upon you for hinting at a proposal of marriage to women, or for concealing it within yourselves. God knows that you will mention them, but do not make promises to them in secret, except to speak in a right manner. Do not resolve upon the marriage bond until the prescribed term reaches its end. Know that God knows what is within yourselves, so beware of Him; and know that God is Forgiving, Forbearing.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The necessary ellipsis","html":"<p>I say: “Those who die” is the subject (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مبتدأ<\/bdi>), and “shall wait” the predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبر<\/bdi>). An ellipsis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حذف<\/bdi>) must be supplied for the predication to be sound, either at the beginning or at the end: “The wives of those who die …,” or “Those who die—their wives shall wait …”.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Those who die” is the subject ( مبتدأ ), and “shall wait” the predicate ( خبر ). An ellipsis ( حذف ) must be supplied for the predication to be sound, either at the beginning or at the end: “The wives of those who die …,” or “Those who die—their wives shall wait …”."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The widow’s waiting period","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those of you who die, believers, and leave wives—the wives shall not marry until they have waited by themselves for four months and ten days. This is because a male foetus generally moves after three months, and a female after four; ten days are added as a precaution. This concerns the woman who is not pregnant. A pregnant woman’s waiting period ends when she gives birth.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-181\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B175]<\/a><\/p><p>When their waiting period expires, there is no blame upon you, guardians, concerning their adorning themselves, making themselves available for marriage or marrying, in a right manner: an adornment or marriage not disapproved by the Sacred Law. God is aware of what you do and will recompense you for what you have done.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those of you who die, believers, and leave wives—the wives shall not marry until they have waited by themselves for four months and ten days. This is because a male foetus generally moves after three months, and a female after four; ten days are added as a precaution. This concerns the woman who is not pregnant. A pregnant woman’s waiting period ends when she gives birth. [B175] When their waiting period expires, there is no blame upon you, guardians, concerning their adorning themselves, making themselves available for marriage or marrying, in a right manner: an adornment or marriage not disapproved by the Sacred Law. God is aware of what you do and will recompense you for what you have done."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Allusion to marriage without a premature contract","html":"<p>There is no blame upon you, suitors, for hinting at a proposal to women in their waiting period. A man might say: “I am desirous of your companionship”; “I wish to marry at this time”; “You are much sought after”; or “It is not fitting that you remain without a husband”—and similar expressions. Nor is there blame for concealing within yourselves during the waiting period a wish to marry, without declaring it openly. God knows that, out of eagerness and desire, you will mention women in their waiting period and discuss marrying them. You may therefore allude to this, but do not make promises to them in secret—that is, in private—or promise marriage or intercourse, except to speak in a right manner through the indirect expressions already mentioned.<\/p><p>Do not conclude the marriage bond or determine to do so until the woman’s prescribed term has ended and the waiting period expired. Know that God knows your desire and eagerness, so beware of Him. Someone’s eagerness and desire for a thing before its time may be punished by being deprived of it. What has been allotted to you will not belong to another, and what belongs to another will not be yours, whatever you do. Know that God is Forgiving concerning your haste—for the human being was created hasty—and Forbearing: He does not hasten against you or expose your secrets.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"There is no blame upon you, suitors, for hinting at a proposal to women in their waiting period. A man might say: “I am desirous of your companionship”; “I wish to marry at this time”; “You are much sought after”; or “It is not fitting that you remain without a husband”—and similar expressions. Nor is there blame for concealing within yourselves during the waiting period a wish to marry, without declaring it openly. God knows that, out of eagerness and desire, you will mention women in their waiting period and discuss marrying them. You may therefore allude to this, but do not make promises to them in secret—that is, in private—or promise marriage or intercourse, except to speak in a right manner through the indirect expressions already mentioned. Do not conclude the marriage bond or determine to do so until the woman’s prescribed term has ended and the waiting period expired. Know that God knows your desire and eagerness, so beware of Him. Someone’s eagerness and desire for a thing before its time may be punished by being deprived of it. What has been allotted to you will not belong to another, and what belongs to another will not be yours, whatever you do. Know that God is Forgiving concerning your haste—for the human being was created hasty—and Forbearing: He does not hasten against you or expose your secrets."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Do not seek the sciences of Reality before their time","html":"<p>When the self dies to desire and abandons its interests and appetites, it should not be returned to them until it has waited for a time, so that the effects of renunciation appear in it: resting in God and finding intimacy in witnessing Him, until it is absent from everything other than Him. When it attains this quality, there is no blame upon the disciple for allowing it what it does in a right manner, without excess or inclination towards desire. Its action is then through God, from God and towards God. God is aware of what you do: nothing concerning it is hidden from Him.<\/p><p>There is no blame upon you, disciples, when your selves have been purified and your hearts cleansed of other things, for hinting at a proposal to the virgins of the realities and the previously wedded women of the sciences, or for concealing within yourselves gnoses and understanding. God knows that you will mention these with the tongue before their taste has reached the heart. Do not therefore declare the sciences of Reality openly to everyone, for that is the conduct of the heretics (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الزنادقة<\/bdi>), except to speak in a right manner, through indication or allusion. Our knowledge is all allusion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إشارة<\/bdi>); when it becomes explicit expression (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عبارة<\/bdi>), it is concealed.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-182\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B176]<\/a><\/p><p>Do not seek the knowledge of realities before its appointed time: the death of selves, renunciation of money, complete nurture and perfected purification. Know that God knows your eagerness for it before its time, so beware lest He punish you by withholding it. Know that God is Forgiving, Forbearing: He does not hasten to deprive you of your aim, if your purpose is sound. And God Most High knows best; and success is through God.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"When the self dies to desire and abandons its interests and appetites, it should not be returned to them until it has waited for a time, so that the effects of renunciation appear in it: resting in God and finding intimacy in witnessing Him, until it is absent from everything other than Him. When it attains this quality, there is no blame upon the disciple for allowing it what it does in a right manner, without excess or inclination towards desire. Its action is then through God, from God and towards God. God is aware of what you do: nothing concerning it is hidden from Him. There is no blame upon you, disciples, when your selves have been purified and your hearts cleansed of other things, for hinting at a proposal to the virgins of the realities and the previously wedded women of the sciences, or for concealing within yourselves gnoses and understanding. God knows that you will mention these with the tongue before their taste has reached the heart. Do not therefore declare the sciences of Reality openly to everyone, for that is the conduct of the heretics ( الزنادقة ), except to speak in a right manner, through indication or allusion. Our knowledge is all allusion ( إشارة ); when it becomes explicit expression ( عبارة ), it is concealed. [B176] Do not seek the knowledge of realities before its appointed time: the death of selves, renunciation of money, complete nurture and perfected purification. Know that God knows your eagerness for it before its time, so beware lest He punish you by withholding it. Know that God is Forgiving, Forbearing: He does not hasten to deprive you of your aim, if your purpose is sound. And God Most High knows best; and success is through God."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, then mentions divorce before consummation.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, then mentions divorce before consummation."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C263.1","page":263,"ar":"ذكر ذلك البيضاوي، في أنوار التنزيل. وفيه مخالفة للحديث المتفق عليه: (إن أحدكم يجمع خلقه في بطن أمه...) الحديث إلى قوله صلّى الله عليه وسلم: (ثم يرسل الملك، فينفخ فيه الروح...) وظاهر الحديث يفيد: أن نفخ الروح بعد هذه المدة مطلقا، لا فرق بين ذكر وأنثى. راجع تفسير الألوسي.","en":"Al-Bayḍāwī mentions this in Anwār al-tanzīl. It conflicts with the agreed-upon hadith, “The creation of each of you is brought together in his mother’s womb …,” continuing to the Prophet’s words, “Then the angel is sent and breathes the spirit into him ….” The apparent sense of the hadith indicates that the breathing of the spirit occurs after this period without qualification, with no distinction between male and female. Consult al-Ālūsī’s commentary.","anchor":"إن كان أنثى في الغالب"},{"id":"C263.2","page":263,"ar":"نافقة أي: مرغوب فيها.","en":"Nāfiqa: that is, sought after.","anchor":"وإنك لنافقة"}],"english_characters":4126,"arabic_characters":3569},{"id":"q2-236-237","q":"2:236–237","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:236–237 · Divorce before consummation, compensation and generosity","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 264–266","cairo":"I:264–266","source_page_first":264,"source_page_last":266,"ar":"لا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ إِنْ طَلَّقْتُمُ النِّساءَ ما لَمْ تَمَسُّوهُنَّ أَوْ تَفْرِضُوا لَهُنَّ فَرِيضَةً وَمَتِّعُوهُنَّ عَلَى الْمُوسِعِ قَدَرُهُ وَعَلَى الْمُقْتِرِ قَدَرُهُ مَتاعاً بِالْمَعْرُوفِ حَقًّا عَلَى الْمُحْسِنِينَ (236) وَإِنْ طَلَّقْتُمُوهُنَّ مِنْ قَبْلِ أَنْ تَمَسُّوهُنَّ وَقَدْ فَرَضْتُمْ لَهُنَّ فَرِيضَةً فَنِصْفُ ما فَرَضْتُمْ إِلاَّ أَنْ يَعْفُونَ أَوْ يَعْفُوَا الَّذِي بِيَدِهِ عُقْدَةُ النِّكاحِ وَأَنْ تَعْفُوا أَقْرَبُ لِلتَّقْوى وَلا تَنْسَوُا الْفَضْلَ بَيْنَكُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ بَصِيرٌ (237)\nقلت: (ما) مصدرية ظرفية، وأَوْ تَفْرِضُوا معطوف على تَمَسُّوهُنَّ أي: لا تبعة عليكم ولا إثم إن طلقتم النساء قبل البناء، مدة كونكم لم تمسوهن ولم تفرضوا لهن مهرا، وإِلَّا أَنْ يَعْفُونَ مبني لاتصاله بنون النسوة، ووزنه: يفعُلن كقوله تعالى: السِّجْنُ أَحَبُّ إِلَيَّ مِمَّا يَدْعُونَنِي إِلَيْهِ وقوله وَالْقَواعِدُ مِنَ النِّساءِ اللَّاتِي لا يَرْجُونَ نِكاحاً، وحَقًّا مفعول مطلق.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: لا حرج عليكم من إثم أو صداق، إِنْ طَلَّقْتُمُ النِّساءَ مدة كونكم لَمْ تَمَسُّوهُنَّ بالجماع، ولَم تَفْرِضُوا لَهُنَّ فَرِيضَةً من الصداق، فطلقوهن حينئذٍ، وَمَتِّعُوهُنَّ أي: أعطوهن ما يتمتعن به ويجبر كسرهن، على قدر حال الزوج عَلَى الْمُوسِعِ أي: الغنى، قَدَرُهُ من المتعة كأمة أو كسوة أو مال يليق بحاله، وَعَلَى الْمُقْتِرِ أي: الذي تقتر رزقَه، أي ضَيّق عليه، وهو الفقير، قَدَرُهُ ما يقدر عليه، فمتعوهن مَتاعاً بِالْمَعْرُوفِ من غير سَرَفٍ ولا تقتير، حَقًّا عَلَى الْمُحْسِنِينَ أي: حقّ ذلك عليهم حقّاً. حمل مالكُ الأمْرَ على الندب، وحمله غيره على الوجوب، وهو الظاهر.\nوإن طلقتموهن بعد المسيس فالصداق كامل، وَإِنْ طَلَّقْتُمُوهُنَّ مِنْ قَبْلِ أَنْ تَمَسُّوهُنَّ وَقَدْ فَرَضْتُمْ لَهُنَّ صداقاً فَنِصْفُ ما فَرَضْتُمْ يجب عليكم، إِلَّا أَنْ يَعْفُونَ أي: النساء، عن نصف الصداق، أَوْ يَعْفُوَا الَّذِي بِيَدِهِ عُقْدَةُ النِّكاحِ، وهو الأب في ابنته البكر قاله مالك، أو الزوج بأن يدفعه كاملاً، قاله الشافعي، وَأَنْ تَعْفُوا أيها الأولياء عن الزوج، فلا تقبضوا منه شيئاً، أَقْرَبُ لِلتَّقْوى لأن المرأة لم يذهب لها شيء فسلُعتُها قائمة، وَلا تَنْسَوُا الْفَضْلَ والإحسان بَيْنَكُمْ، فتسامحوا يسمح لكم، إِنَّ اللَّهَ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ بَصِيرٌ لا يخفى عليه شيء من أعمالكم، فيجازي المحسن بإحسانه، وَاللَّهُ يُحِبُّ الْمُحْسِنِينَ.\nالإشارة: من المريدين من تحصل له الغيبة عن نفسه، والجذب عنها، بعد أن يمسها بالمجاهدة والمكابدة، فحينئذٍ يمتعها بالشهود والعيان، وهذه طريق الجادة. ومنهم من تحصل له الغيبة عن نفسه والجذب عنها قبل أن يمسها، ويجاهدها، وهو نادر بالنسبة إلى الأول، فيقال لهؤلاء الفريق: لا جناح عليكم إن طلقتم أنفسكم، وغبتم عنها، من قبل أن تمسوها، وقبل أن تعرضوا عليها وظائف العبودية. ومتعوهن بالشهود والعيان على قدر وسعكم وقوة شهودكم، على الموسع قدره من لذة الشهود، وعلى المقتر- أي: المضيق عليه في المعرفة- قدره من لذة الشهود، حق ذلك حقا على المحسنين الذين حازوا مقام الإحسان، وفازوا بالشهود والعيان.\nوإن حصل لكم جذب العناية، وطلقتم أنفسكم قبل أن تمسوها، وقد كنتم وظفتم عليها أوراداً من وظائف العبودية فنصف ما فرضتم، وهو المهم منها لأن عبادتها صارت قلبية، فيكفيها من العبادة القالبية المهم، إلا أن تقوى على ذلك مع الشهود. أو يأمرها الذي بيده عقدة نكاحها، وهو الشيخ، فلا يضرها الاشتغال بها حيث كان بإذْن، وأن تعفوا، أيها الشيوخ، عن المريدين في العبادة الحسيّة، وتأمروهم بالعبادة القلبية، أقرب للتقوى الكاملة، وهي تقوى السَّوَى. والله تعالى أعلم.\nولما ذكر الحق تعالى شأن النساء، حذر من الاشتغال بهن عن العبادة، فقال:","quran_ar":"لا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ إِنْ طَلَّقْتُمُ النِّساءَ ما لَمْ تَمَسُّوهُنَّ أَوْ تَفْرِضُوا لَهُنَّ فَرِيضَةً وَمَتِّعُوهُنَّ عَلَى الْمُوسِعِ قَدَرُهُ وَعَلَى الْمُقْتِرِ قَدَرُهُ مَتاعاً بِالْمَعْرُوفِ حَقًّا عَلَى الْمُحْسِنِينَ (236) وَإِنْ طَلَّقْتُمُوهُنَّ مِنْ قَبْلِ أَنْ تَمَسُّوهُنَّ وَقَدْ فَرَضْتُمْ لَهُنَّ فَرِيضَةً فَنِصْفُ ما فَرَضْتُمْ إِلاَّ أَنْ يَعْفُونَ أَوْ يَعْفُوَا الَّذِي بِيَدِهِ عُقْدَةُ النِّكاحِ وَأَنْ تَعْفُوا أَقْرَبُ لِلتَّقْوى وَلا تَنْسَوُا الْفَضْلَ بَيْنَكُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ بَصِيرٌ (237)","quran_en":"236. There is no blame upon you if you divorce women before you have touched them or assigned them a dower. Give them a provision: the affluent according to his means and the straitened according to his means—a provision in a right manner, a duty upon those who do good. 237. If you divorce them before you have touched them, but have assigned them a dower, then half of what you assigned is due, unless they remit it or the one who holds the marriage bond remits it. To remit is nearer to reverence. Do not forget generosity between you. God sees what you do.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The temporal construction and the feminine plural","html":"<p>I say: <i>mā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ما<\/bdi>) is infinitival and temporal (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدرية ظرفية<\/bdi>). “Or assign” is coordinated with “touch”: you incur neither liability nor sin by divorcing women before consummation, during the period in which you have neither touched them nor assigned them a dower.<\/p><p>“Unless they remit” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إلا أن يعفون<\/bdi>) is indeclinable because of its attachment to the feminine plural <i>nūn<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نون النسوة<\/bdi>). Its pattern is <i>yafʿulna<\/i>, like “Prison is dearer to me than that to which they invite me” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يدعونني<\/bdi>) [12:33], and “The women past childbearing who do not hope for marriage” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لا يرجون نكاحاً<\/bdi>) [24:60]. “A duty” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حقاً<\/bdi>) is an absolute accusative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول مطلق<\/bdi>).<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-183\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B177]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: mā ( ما ) is infinitival and temporal ( مصدرية ظرفية ). “Or assign” is coordinated with “touch”: you incur neither liability nor sin by divorcing women before consummation, during the period in which you have neither touched them nor assigned them a dower. “Unless they remit” ( إلا أن يعفون ) is indeclinable because of its attachment to the feminine plural nūn ( نون النسوة ). Its pattern is yafʿulna , like “Prison is dearer to me than that to which they invite me” ( يدعونني ) [12:33], and “The women past childbearing who do not hope for marriage” ( لا يرجون نكاحاً ) [24:60]. “A duty” ( حقاً ) is an absolute accusative ( مفعول مطلق ). [B177]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The compensatory gift and the assigned dower","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: there is no liability upon you in respect of sin or dower if you divorce women while you have neither touched them through intercourse nor assigned them a dower. You may then divorce them, but give them a provision: something from which they can benefit and which mends their brokenness, according to the husband’s circumstances. The affluent gives according to his means—a slave woman, clothing or money appropriate to his circumstances. The straitened man, whose livelihood has been restricted, gives what he can afford. Give them a provision in a right manner, without extravagance or stinting. It is a duty upon those who do good: it is truly due from them. Mālik understood the command as recommendation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ندب<\/bdi>), while others understood it as obligation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وجوب<\/bdi>); the latter is the apparent meaning.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-184\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B178]<\/a><\/p><p>If you divorce them after consummation, the full dower is due. If you divorce them before touching them but have assigned them a dower, half of what you assigned is due from you, unless the women remit their half, or the one who holds the marriage bond remits it. According to Mālik, this is the father in the case of his virgin daughter. According to al-Shāfiʿī, it is the husband, by paying the whole dower.<\/p><p>For you, guardians, to remit the husband’s payment and take nothing from him is nearer to reverence, since the woman has lost nothing and her “goods” remain intact. Do not forget generosity and kindness between you. Be forbearing, and forbearance will be shown to you. God sees what you do; none of your deeds is hidden from Him. He recompenses the one who does good for his goodness, and God loves those who do good.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-185\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B178]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: there is no liability upon you in respect of sin or dower if you divorce women while you have neither touched them through intercourse nor assigned them a dower. You may then divorce them, but give them a provision: something from which they can benefit and which mends their brokenness, according to the husband’s circumstances. The affluent gives according to his means—a slave woman, clothing or money appropriate to his circumstances. The straitened man, whose livelihood has been restricted, gives what he can afford. Give them a provision in a right manner, without extravagance or stinting. It is a duty upon those who do good: it is truly due from them. Mālik understood the command as recommendation ( ندب ), while others understood it as obligation ( وجوب ); the latter is the apparent meaning. [B178] If you divorce them after consummation, the full dower is due. If you divorce them before touching them but have assigned them a dower, half of what you assigned is due from you, unless the women remit their half, or the one who holds the marriage bond remits it. According to Mālik, this is the father in the case of his virgin daughter. According to al-Shāfiʿī, it is the husband, by paying the whole dower. For you, guardians, to remit the husband’s payment and take nothing from him is nearer to reverence, since the woman has lost nothing and her “goods” remain intact. Do not forget generosity and kindness between you. Be forbearing, and forbearance will be shown to you. God sees what you do; none of your deeds is hidden from Him. He recompenses the one who does good for his goodness, and God loves those who do good. [B178]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Attraction before or after discipline","html":"<p>Some disciples attain absence from their selves and attraction away from them (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجذب<\/bdi>) after having touched them with struggle and endurance. They then allow them the enjoyment of witnessing and direct vision (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشهود والعيان<\/bdi>). This is the usual path. Others attain absence from the self and attraction away from it before touching it or struggling against it; this is rare compared with the first. To this latter group it is said: there is no blame upon you for divorcing your selves and becoming absent from them before touching them and before imposing upon them the observances of servanthood (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وظائف العبودية<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>Allow them the enjoyment of witnessing and direct vision according to your capacity and the strength of your witnessing. The one granted amplitude receives the delight of witnessing according to his capacity; the one whose gnosis remains restricted receives its delight according to his capacity. This is truly due from those who do good: those who have attained the station of spiritual excellence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مقام الإحسان<\/bdi>) and gained witnessing and direct vision.<\/p><p>If the attraction of divine care reaches you and you divorce your selves before touching them, having already imposed upon them litanies (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أوراد<\/bdi>) among the observances of servanthood, then retain half of what you imposed: the essential part. Their worship has become inward, of the heart (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قلبية<\/bdi>), and the essential bodily worship (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قالبية<\/bdi>) suffices them—unless they are strong enough to combine the whole with witnessing, or the one who holds their marriage bond, the shaykh, commands them to do so. Occupation with these observances does not harm them when undertaken with permission.<\/p><p>For you, shaykhs, to remit some sensory worship from the disciples and direct them to worship of the heart is nearer to complete reverence: guarding against everything other than God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تقوى السوى<\/bdi>). And God Most High knows best.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-186\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B179]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Some disciples attain absence from their selves and attraction away from them ( الجذب ) after having touched them with struggle and endurance. They then allow them the enjoyment of witnessing and direct vision ( الشهود والعيان ). This is the usual path. Others attain absence from the self and attraction away from it before touching it or struggling against it; this is rare compared with the first. To this latter group it is said: there is no blame upon you for divorcing your selves and becoming absent from them before touching them and before imposing upon them the observances of servanthood ( وظائف العبودية ). Allow them the enjoyment of witnessing and direct vision according to your capacity and the strength of your witnessing. The one granted amplitude receives the delight of witnessing according to his capacity; the one whose gnosis remains restricted receives its delight according to his capacity. This is truly due from those who do good: those who have attained the station of spiritual excellence ( مقام الإحسان ) and gained witnessing and direct vision. If the attraction of divine care reaches you and you divorce your selves before touching them, having already imposed upon them litanies ( أوراد ) among the observances of servanthood, then retain half of what you imposed: the essential part. Their worship has become inward, of the heart ( قلبية ), and the essential bodily worship ( قالبية ) suffices them—unless they are strong enough to combine the whole with witnessing, or the one who holds their marriage bond, the shaykh, commands them to do so. Occupation with these observances does not harm them when undertaken with permission. For you, shaykhs, to remit some sensory worship from the disciples and direct them to worship of the heart is nearer to complete reverence: guarding against everything other than God ( تقوى السوى ). And God Most High knows best. [B179]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Having mentioned the affairs of women, the Real Most High warns against allowing them to distract one from worship.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Having mentioned the affairs of women, the Real Most High warns against allowing them to distract one from worship."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4358,"arabic_characters":3249},{"id":"q2-238-239","q":"2:238–239","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:238–239 · Guard the prayers, in security and in fear","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 266–267","cairo":"I:266–267","source_page_first":266,"source_page_last":267,"ar":"حافِظُوا عَلَى الصَّلَواتِ وَالصَّلاةِ الْوُسْطى وَقُومُوا لِلَّهِ قانِتِينَ (238) فَإِنْ خِفْتُمْ فَرِجالاً أَوْ رُكْباناً فَإِذا أَمِنْتُمْ فَاذْكُرُوا اللَّهَ كَما عَلَّمَكُمْ ما لَمْ تَكُونُوا تَعْلَمُونَ (239)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: حافِظُوا أيضاً على أداء الصَّلَواتِ الخمس في أوقاتها بإتقان شروطها وأركانها وخشوعها وآدابها، ولا تشتغلوا عنها بشهوات النساء وتشغيب أحكامهن، ولا بغير ذلك، وحافظوا أيضاً على الصَّلاةِ الْوُسْطى وهى العصر عند الشافعي، وهو ظاهر الحديث، أو الصبح عند مالك لفضلها، أو لتوسطها بين صلاتي الليل والنهار. وما من صلاة إلا وقيل فيها الوسطى. وقيل: أخفيت كساعة الجمعة وليلة القدر.\nوَقُومُوا لِلَّهِ في الصلاة قانِتِينَ أي: ساكتين، وكان، قبل نزول الآية، الكلام في الصلاة جائزاً، أو قيل: مطيعين. إذ القنوت في القرآن كله بمعنى الطاعة. فَإِنْ خِفْتُمْ من عدو، أو سَبع، أو سَيْل، فصلُّوا قياماً على أرجلكم بالإيماء للسجود، أَوْ رُكْباناً على خيولكم بالإيماء للركوع والسجود، فَإِذا أَمِنْتُمْ في الصلاة، أو بعدها، فصلوا صلاة أمن، فَاذْكُرُوا اللَّهَ في الصلاة، وصلوا كَما عَلَّمَكُمْ من الكيفية ما لَمْ تَكُونُوا تَعْلَمُونَ قبل ذلك.\nالإشارة: حافظوا على الصلوات الحسية قياماً بوظائف العبودية، وعلى الصلاة القلبية قياماً بشهود عظمة الربوبية وهي الصلاة الوسطى لدوامها في كل ساعة، قيل لبعضهم: هل للقلوب صلاة؟ قال: نعم، إذا سجد لا يرفع رأسه أبداً. هـ. أي: إذا خضع لهيبة العظمة لم يرفع أبداً، وفي ذلك يقول الشاعر:\nفاسجُدْ لهيبةِ الجَلالِ ... عنْد التَّدَانِي\nوَلْتَقْرَأ آيةَ الكَمالِ ... سَبْعَ المَثَانِي\nوأشار بقوله «آية الكمال» لقوله تعالى: إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ ليجمع بين الشريعة والحقيقة، فسجود القلب حقيقة، وسجود الجوارح شريعة، وقوموا لله بآداب العبودية قانتين خاشعين، فإن خفتم ألا تصلوا إلى ربكم، قبل انقضاء أجلكم، فسيروا إليه رجالاً أو ركباناً، خفافاً أو ثقالاً، فإذا أمنتم من القطيعة- وذلك بعد التمكين- فاذكروا الله شكراً لأجل ما أطلعكم عليه، وعلَّمكم ما لم تكونوا تعلمون من عظمة الربوبية، وكمال آداب العبودية.\nثم رجع الحقّ تعالى إلى الكلام على النساء، فقال:","quran_ar":"حافِظُوا عَلَى الصَّلَواتِ وَالصَّلاةِ الْوُسْطى وَقُومُوا لِلَّهِ قانِتِينَ (238) فَإِنْ خِفْتُمْ فَرِجالاً أَوْ رُكْباناً فَإِذا أَمِنْتُمْ فَاذْكُرُوا اللَّهَ كَما عَلَّمَكُمْ ما لَمْ تَكُونُوا تَعْلَمُونَ (239)","quran_en":"238. Guard the prayers, and the middle prayer, and stand before God in devotion. 239. If you are afraid, then on foot or mounted. When you are secure, remember God as He taught you what you did not know.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The appointed prayers and prayer in danger","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: guard also the performance of the five prayers at their appointed times, perfecting their conditions, essential acts, humility and proper manners (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شروطها وأركانها وخشوعها وآدابها<\/bdi>). Do not let desire for women, the troublesome disputes concerning their affairs, or anything else distract you from them. Guard especially the middle prayer. According to al-Shāfiʿī, it is the afternoon prayer, as the apparent meaning of the hadith indicates. According to Mālik, it is the dawn prayer, because of its excellence or its position between the two night prayers and the daytime prayers. Every prayer has been identified as the middle prayer. It is also said that it was concealed, like the hour of Friday and the Night of Power.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-187\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B180]<\/a><\/p><p>Stand before God in prayer <i>qānitīn<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قانتين<\/bdi>): silent. Before this verse was revealed, speaking in prayer was permissible. Alternatively, it means obedient, for <i>qunūt<\/i> throughout the Qurʾān signifies obedience. If you fear an enemy, a wild beast or a flood, pray standing on your feet, gesturing for prostration, or mounted on your horses, gesturing for bowing and prostration. When you become secure, during the prayer or afterwards, perform the prayer of security. Remember God in prayer and pray in the manner He taught you, which you did not previously know.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: guard also the performance of the five prayers at their appointed times, perfecting their conditions, essential acts, humility and proper manners ( شروطها وأركانها وخشوعها وآدابها ). Do not let desire for women, the troublesome disputes concerning their affairs, or anything else distract you from them. Guard especially the middle prayer. According to al-Shāfiʿī, it is the afternoon prayer, as the apparent meaning of the hadith indicates. According to Mālik, it is the dawn prayer, because of its excellence or its position between the two night prayers and the daytime prayers. Every prayer has been identified as the middle prayer. It is also said that it was concealed, like the hour of Friday and the Night of Power. [B180] Stand before God in prayer qānitīn ( قانتين ): silent. Before this verse was revealed, speaking in prayer was permissible. Alternatively, it means obedient, for qunūt throughout the Qurʾān signifies obedience. If you fear an enemy, a wild beast or a flood, pray standing on your feet, gesturing for prostration, or mounted on your horses, gesturing for bowing and prostration. When you become secure, during the prayer or afterwards, perform the prayer of security. Remember God in prayer and pray in the manner He taught you, which you did not previously know."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The prayer of the heart and the prayer of the limbs","html":"<p>Guard the sensory prayers, fulfilling the observances of servanthood, and the prayer of the heart, witnessing the majesty of Lordship. The latter is the middle prayer because it continues at every hour. Someone was asked: “Do hearts have a prayer?” He replied: “Yes. When the heart prostrates, it never raises its head again.” That is, once it has bowed before the awe of divine majesty, it never rises. The poet says concerning this:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Prostrate before the awe of Majesty<br>at the drawing near;<br>and recite the verse of perfection,<br>the Seven Oft-Repeated.<\/blockquote><p>By “the verse of perfection,” he indicates: “You alone we worship, and from You alone we seek help,” joining Sacred Law and Reality. The prostration of the heart is Reality; the prostration of the limbs is Sacred Law. Stand before God observing the manners of servanthood, devoted and humble.<\/p><p>If you fear that you will not reach your Lord before your allotted term expires, travel towards Him on foot or mounted, lightly or heavily laden. When you become secure from severance—and this is after firm establishment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التمكين<\/bdi>)—remember God in gratitude for what He has disclosed to you and taught you, which you did not know: the majesty of Lordship and the perfection of the manners of servanthood.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Guard the sensory prayers, fulfilling the observances of servanthood, and the prayer of the heart, witnessing the majesty of Lordship. The latter is the middle prayer because it continues at every hour. Someone was asked: “Do hearts have a prayer?” He replied: “Yes. When the heart prostrates, it never raises its head again.” That is, once it has bowed before the awe of divine majesty, it never rises. The poet says concerning this: Prostrate before the awe of Majesty at the drawing near; and recite the verse of perfection, the Seven Oft-Repeated. By “the verse of perfection,” he indicates: “You alone we worship, and from You alone we seek help,” joining Sacred Law and Reality. The prostration of the heart is Reality; the prostration of the limbs is Sacred Law. Stand before God observing the manners of servanthood, devoted and humble. If you fear that you will not reach your Lord before your allotted term expires, travel towards Him on foot or mounted, lightly or heavily laden. When you become secure from severance—and this is after firm establishment ( التمكين )—remember God in gratitude for what He has disclosed to you and taught you, which you did not know: the majesty of Lordship and the perfection of the manners of servanthood."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High then returns to the discussion of women.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High then returns to the discussion of women."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2642,"arabic_characters":1897},{"id":"q2-240","q":"2:240","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:240 · The widow’s provision and the preservation of spiritual knowledge","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 267–267","cairo":"I:267","source_page_first":267,"source_page_last":267,"ar":"وَالَّذِينَ يُتَوَفَّوْنَ مِنْكُمْ وَيَذَرُونَ أَزْواجاً وَصِيَّةً لِأَزْواجِهِمْ مَتاعاً إِلَى الْحَوْلِ غَيْرَ إِخْراجٍ فَإِنْ خَرَجْنَ فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ فِي ما فَعَلْنَ فِي أَنْفُسِهِنَّ مِنْ مَعْرُوفٍ وَاللَّهُ عَزِيزٌ حَكِيمٌ (240)\nقلت: (وصية) : مبتدأ، والخبر محذوف، أي: عليهم وصية، ومَنْ نَصَبَ، فمفعول مطلق، أي: فليوصوا وصية، و (غير) : حال من الأزواج، أي: حَالَ كَوْنِهِنَّ غَيْرَ مُخْرَجَاتِ.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَالَّذِينَ يُتَوَفَّوْنَ مِنْكُمْ ويتركون أَزْواجاً بعدهم، فيجب عليهم أن يوصوا لأزواجهم وصية يتمتعن بها من كسوة ونفقة وسكنى، إلى تمام الْحَوْلِ ما دام الأزواج لم يخرجن من مسكن الزوج، فَإِنْ خَرَجْنَ بأنفسهن، فلا نفقة ولا كسوة ولا سكنى عليكم أيها الأولياء، ولا حرج عليكم فِيما فَعَلْنَ فِي أَنْفُسِهِنَّ من التزين والتعرض للنكاح بعد تمام عدتهن، على ما هو معروف في الشرع، والوصية منسوخة بآية الميراث، وتربص الحول بآية أَرْبَعَةَ أَشْهُرٍ وَعَشْراً المتقدمة المتأخرة في النزول(1)، وَاللَّهُ عَزِيزٌ حَكِيمٌ ينسخ ما يشاء، ويحكم ما يريد، باعتبار الحكمة والمصلحة.\nالإشارة: والذين يُتوفون عن الحظوظ والشهوات، ويتركون علوماً وأسراراً، ينبغي لهم أن يوصوا بحفظها وتدوينها، كان الشيخ أبو الحسن الشاذلي رضي الله عنه إذا استغرق في الكلام وفاضت عليه المواهب، يقول: (هلاَّ رجلٌ يقيد عنا هذه العلوم) . هـ. ليقع التمتع بها للسائرين والطالبين، (غير إخراج) لغير أهلها، فإن قضى الوقت بخروجها، من غير قصد، فلا حرج، إما لغلبة وجد أو هداية مريد، (والله عزيز حكيم) ، فعزته اقتضت الغيرة على سره: أن يأخذه غير أهله، وحكمته اقتضت ظهوره في وقته لأهله. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم كرر أمر المتعة تأكيدا، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَالَّذِينَ يُتَوَفَّوْنَ مِنْكُمْ وَيَذَرُونَ أَزْواجاً وَصِيَّةً لِأَزْواجِهِمْ مَتاعاً إِلَى الْحَوْلِ غَيْرَ إِخْراجٍ فَإِنْ خَرَجْنَ فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ فِي ما فَعَلْنَ فِي أَنْفُسِهِنَّ مِنْ مَعْرُوفٍ وَاللَّهُ عَزِيزٌ حَكِيمٌ (240)","quran_en":"240. Those of you who die and leave wives behind shall make a bequest for their wives: provision for a year without being turned out. If they leave, there is no blame upon you concerning what they do with themselves in a right manner. God is Mighty, Wise.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The bequest and the circumstantial expression","html":"<p>I say: in the nominative reading, “a bequest” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وصية<\/bdi>) is the subject, with its predicate omitted: “A bequest is incumbent upon them.” In the accusative reading, it is an absolute accusative: “Let them make a bequest.” “Without being turned out” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غير إخراج<\/bdi>) is a circumstantial expression (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال<\/bdi>) referring to the wives: while they are not being expelled.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: in the nominative reading, “a bequest” ( وصية ) is the subject, with its predicate omitted: “A bequest is incumbent upon them.” In the accusative reading, it is an absolute accusative: “Let them make a bequest.” “Without being turned out” ( غير إخراج ) is a circumstantial expression ( حال ) referring to the wives: while they are not being expelled."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The year’s provision and the account of abrogation","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those of you who die and leave wives behind must make a bequest for their wives, by which they can enjoy clothing, maintenance and lodging until the completion of a year, so long as the wives do not leave the husband’s dwelling. If they leave of their own accord, no maintenance, clothing or lodging is due from you, guardians. Nor is there blame upon you concerning their adorning themselves and making themselves available for marriage after the completion of their waiting period, in a manner recognised by the Sacred Law.<\/p><p>The bequest was abrogated by the inheritance verse, and the year’s waiting period by “four months and ten days,” which occurs earlier in the arrangement but was revealed later. God is Mighty, Wise: He abrogates what He wills and decrees what He desires, in accordance with wisdom and benefit.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-188\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B181]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those of you who die and leave wives behind must make a bequest for their wives, by which they can enjoy clothing, maintenance and lodging until the completion of a year, so long as the wives do not leave the husband’s dwelling. If they leave of their own accord, no maintenance, clothing or lodging is due from you, guardians. Nor is there blame upon you concerning their adorning themselves and making themselves available for marriage after the completion of their waiting period, in a manner recognised by the Sacred Law. The bequest was abrogated by the inheritance verse, and the year’s waiting period by “four months and ten days,” which occurs earlier in the arrangement but was revealed later. God is Mighty, Wise: He abrogates what He wills and decrees what He desires, in accordance with wisdom and benefit. [B181]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Record the sciences, and guard their proper recipients","html":"<p>Those who die to personal interests and desires, leaving sciences and mysteries behind, should enjoin their preservation and recording. When spiritual gifts poured upon Shaykh Abū al-Ḥasan al-Shādhilī and he became absorbed in speaking, he would say: “Is there no man who will record these sciences from us?” This is so that travellers and seekers may benefit from them, “without being turned out”—not disclosed to those unfit for them.<\/p><p>If the moment decrees that they emerge unintentionally, there is no blame: it may occur through an overpowering spiritual finding (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وجد<\/bdi>) or for a disciple’s guidance. “God is Mighty, Wise.” His might requires jealous protection of His mystery, lest one unfit for it receive it; His wisdom requires its appearance at its proper time to those fit for it. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Those who die to personal interests and desires, leaving sciences and mysteries behind, should enjoin their preservation and recording. When spiritual gifts poured upon Shaykh Abū al-Ḥasan al-Shādhilī and he became absorbed in speaking, he would say: “Is there no man who will record these sciences from us?” This is so that travellers and seekers may benefit from them, “without being turned out”—not disclosed to those unfit for them. If the moment decrees that they emerge unintentionally, there is no blame: it may occur through an overpowering spiritual finding ( وجد ) or for a disciple’s guidance. “God is Mighty, Wise.” His might requires jealous protection of His mystery, lest one unfit for it receive it; His wisdom requires its appearance at its proper time to those fit for it. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then repeats the command to give a provision, for emphasis.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then repeats the command to give a provision, for emphasis."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C267.1","page":267,"ar":"أي: متقدمة في التلاوة.","en":"That is, earlier in the order of recitation.","anchor":"المتقدمة المتأخرة في النزول"}],"english_characters":2105,"arabic_characters":1501},{"id":"q2-241-242","q":"2:241–242","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:241–242 · Provision for divorced women and the sweetness of worship","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 267–268","cairo":"I:267–268","source_page_first":267,"source_page_last":268,"ar":"وَلِلْمُطَلَّقاتِ مَتاعٌ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ حَقًّا عَلَى الْمُتَّقِينَ (241) كَذلِكَ يُبَيِّنُ اللَّهُ لَكُمْ آياتِهِ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَعْقِلُونَ (242)\nقلت: إنما كرره لأن الأولى في غير المدخول بها، إذا طُلقت قبل الفرض، وهذه في المدخول بها، وعبَّر أولاً بالمحسن: لأن المتعة قبل الدخول لا يعطيها إلا أهل الإحسان لأن المطلق لم يحصل له تمتع بالزوجة، بخلاف الثاني، فمطلق المدخول بها، التقوى تحمله على الإمتاع.\nوقيل: لمّا نزلت الآية الأولى، قال رجل من المسلمين: إنْ أحسنتُ مَتَّعْتُ وإلاَّ تَركتُ، فنزلت الثانية تأكيداً.\nوقال: حَقًّا عَلَى الْمُتَّقِينَ الشرك، أي: على كل مؤمن، وحكمها: الندب، عند مالك، على تفصيل ذكره في المختصر، فقال عاطفاً على المندوب: والمتعة على قدر حاله، بعد العدة للرجعة، أو ورثتها، ككل مُطَلَّقة في نكاح لازم، لا في فَسْخ كلعَانٍ وملك أحد الزوجين، إلا من اختلعَتْ، أو فُرض لها وطُلقت قبل البناء، ومختارة لعتقِها أو لِعَيْبه أو مُخَيَّرَة أو مُمَلَّكة.\nالإشارة: كل من طلق نفسه وخالف هواها تمتع بحلاوة المعاملة مع ربه، فمن اتصل بشيخ التربية تمتع بحلاوة العبادة القلبية كالشهود والعيان، ومن لم يتصل بالشيخ تمتع بحلاوة العبادة الحسية. فالآية الأولى في المريدين والواصلين، وهذه الآية في العُبَّاد والزهاد، ولذلك عبّر في الأولى بالمحسنين، وفي الثانية بالمتقين، والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم حذّر من الفرار من الموت، توطئة للترغيب فى الجهاد، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلِلْمُطَلَّقاتِ مَتاعٌ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ حَقًّا عَلَى الْمُتَّقِينَ (241) كَذلِكَ يُبَيِّنُ اللَّهُ لَكُمْ آياتِهِ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَعْقِلُونَ (242)","quran_en":"241. For divorced women there shall be a provision in a right manner, a duty upon those who are mindful of God. 242. Thus God makes His signs clear to you, so that you may understand.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Why the command is repeated","html":"<p>I say: He repeats it because the first verse concerns a woman divorced before consummation and before a dower has been assigned, whereas this concerns one whose marriage has been consummated. In the first, He speaks of “those who do good,” because only people of goodness give this provision before consummation: the divorcing man has received no enjoyment from his wife. In the second case, however, reverence moves the man who divorces a wife after consummation to provide for her.<\/p><p>It is also said that when the first verse was revealed, a Muslim man said: “If I do good, I give her a provision; otherwise I leave it.” The second was therefore revealed as confirmation. It says: “A duty upon those who are mindful”—who guard against associating partners with God; that is, upon every believer.<\/p><p>According to Mālik, its ruling is recommendation, with the distinctions mentioned in the <i>Mukhtaṣar<\/i>. There, in a list of recommended acts, he says: “The consolatory provision is according to his circumstances; in a revocable divorce, after the waiting period, or to her heirs—as for every woman divorced from a binding marriage, not a dissolution, such as mutual imprecation or one spouse’s acquisition of the other. Exceptions are the woman who obtains release by compensation; one assigned a dower and divorced before consummation; and one who chooses separation because of her emancipation, his defect, an option given to her or delegated power of divorce.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-189\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B182]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: He repeats it because the first verse concerns a woman divorced before consummation and before a dower has been assigned, whereas this concerns one whose marriage has been consummated. In the first, He speaks of “those who do good,” because only people of goodness give this provision before consummation: the divorcing man has received no enjoyment from his wife. In the second case, however, reverence moves the man who divorces a wife after consummation to provide for her. It is also said that when the first verse was revealed, a Muslim man said: “If I do good, I give her a provision; otherwise I leave it.” The second was therefore revealed as confirmation. It says: “A duty upon those who are mindful”—who guard against associating partners with God; that is, upon every believer. According to Mālik, its ruling is recommendation, with the distinctions mentioned in the Mukhtaṣar . There, in a list of recommended acts, he says: “The consolatory provision is according to his circumstances; in a revocable divorce, after the waiting period, or to her heirs—as for every woman divorced from a binding marriage, not a dissolution, such as mutual imprecation or one spouse’s acquisition of the other. Exceptions are the woman who obtains release by compensation; one assigned a dower and divorced before consummation; and one who chooses separation because of her emancipation, his defect, an option given to her or delegated power of divorce.” [B182]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Two kinds of sweetness","html":"<p>Everyone who divorces his self and opposes its desire enjoys the sweetness of dealings with his Lord. One who has joined a shaykh of spiritual nurture enjoys the sweetness of inward worship, such as witnessing and direct vision. One who has not joined a shaykh enjoys the sweetness of sensory worship. The first verse thus concerns disciples and those who have arrived, and this verse worshippers and ascetics. Hence the first speaks of “those who do good” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المحسنين<\/bdi>), and the second of “those who are mindful” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المتقين<\/bdi>). And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Everyone who divorces his self and opposes its desire enjoys the sweetness of dealings with his Lord. One who has joined a shaykh of spiritual nurture enjoys the sweetness of inward worship, such as witnessing and direct vision. One who has not joined a shaykh enjoys the sweetness of sensory worship. The first verse thus concerns disciples and those who have arrived, and this verse worshippers and ascetics. Hence the first speaks of “those who do good” ( المحسنين ), and the second of “those who are mindful” ( المتقين ). And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then warns against flight from death, preparing the way for encouragement to struggle in His cause.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then warns against flight from death, preparing the way for encouragement to struggle in His cause."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2120,"arabic_characters":1241},{"id":"q2-243","q":"2:243","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:243 · Flight from death, the plague and life after effacement","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 268–270","cairo":"I:268–270","source_page_first":268,"source_page_last":270,"ar":"أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى الَّذِينَ خَرَجُوا مِنْ دِيارِهِمْ وَهُمْ أُلُوفٌ حَذَرَ الْمَوْتِ فَقالَ لَهُمُ اللَّهُ مُوتُوا ثُمَّ أَحْياهُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ لَذُو فَضْلٍ عَلَى النَّاسِ وَلكِنَّ أَكْثَرَ النَّاسِ لا يَشْكُرُونَ (243)\nقلت: الاستفهام للتعجب والتشويق، والرؤية قلبية، والواو للحال، و (حَذَرَ) مفعول من أجله.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ألم تنظر يا محمد، بعين الفكر والاعتبار، إِلَى الَّذِينَ خَرَجُوا مِنْ دِيارِهِمْ وَهُمْ أُلُوفٌ عشرة، أو ثلاثون، أو أربعون، أو سبعون، حذراً من الْمَوْتِ في زمن الطاعون.\nوكانوا في قرية يقال لها: (داوردان) فلما وقع بها الطاعون، خرجت طائفة هاربين، وبقيت أخرى، فهلك أكثر من بقي، وسلم الخارجون، ثم رجعوا، فقال الباقون: لو صنعنا مثلهم لبقينا، لئن أصابنا الطاعون مرة ثانية لخرجنا، فأصابهم من قابل، فهربوا كلهم، ونزلوا وادياً أفيح(1) ، فناداهم ملك من أسفل الوادي، وآخر من أعلاه، أن:\nموتوا، فماتوا كلهم أجمعون، ومرت عليهم مدة ثمانية أيام أو أكثر حتى انتفخوا، وقيل: صاروا عظاماً، فمرَّ عليهم نبيّ الله (حزقيل) ، فدعا الله تعالى، واستشفع فيهم، فأحياهم الله، وعاشوا دهراً، عليهم سيما الموت لا يلبسون ثوباً إلا عاد كالكفن، واستمر في أسباطهم. هـ.\nقال الأصمعي: لما وقع الطاعون بالبصرة، خرج رجل منها على حمار معه أهله، وله عبد يسوق حماره، فأنشأ العبد يقول:\nلن يُسبَقَ اللهُ على حِمار ... ولا على ذي مَشعَةٍ طيّار\nقد يصبح الله أمَامَ السارِي(1)\nفرجع الرجل بعياله.\nوالآية تدل على أن الفرار من الطاعون حرام في تلك الشريعة، كما حرم في شرعنا، وروى عبد الرحمن ابن عوف أن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم قال: «إِذَا سَمِعْتُمْ هذا الوباء بِبلد فَلاَ تَقْدُمُوا عَلَيْهِ، وإذَا وَقَعَ بِبلد وَأَنْتُمْ فيه فَلاَ تَخْرُجُوا مِنْهُ» .\nقلت: وقد اختلف الأئمة في حكم الفرار والقدوم: فمنهم من شهر المنع فيهما تمسكاً بظاهر الحديث، ومنهم من شهر الكراهة. والمختار في الفرار: التحريم، وفي القدوم: التفصيل، فمن قوي يقينه، وصفا توحيده، حَلَّ له القدوم، ومن ضعف يقينه، بحيث إذا أصابه شيء نسب التأثير لغير الله حرم عليه القدوم.\nوفي حديث عائشة- رضي الله عنها- قلت: يا رسول الله، ما الطاعون؟ قال: «غدة كغدة البعير، المقيم فيه كالشهيد، والفارُّ منه كالفار من الزحْف» . قال ابن حجر: كون المقيم فيه له أجر شهيد إنما بشرط أن يعلم أنه لا يصيبه إلا ما كتب الله له، وأن يسلم إليه أمره ويرضى بقضائه، وأن يبقى في مكانه ولا يخرج منه بقصد الفرار، فإذا اتصف الجالس بهذه القيود حصل له أجر الشهادة. ودخل تحته ثلاث صور، الأولى: من اتصف بذلك فوقع له الطاعون ومات فهو شهيد. والثانية: من وقع به ولم يمت به فهو شهيد وإن مات بعد ذلك. والثالثة: من لم يقع به أصلاً ومات بغيره عاجلاً أو آجلاً فهو شهيد، إذا حصلت فيه القيود الثلاثة، ومن لم يتصف بالقيود الثلاثة فليس بشهيد، ولو مات بالطاعون. والله أعلم هـ.\nوأما القدوم من بلد الطاعون إلى البلد السالمة منه فجائز. ولا يُمنع من الدخول، قاله الباجي وابن حجر والحطاب وغيرهم لقوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «لاَ عَدْوَى وَلاَ طِيَرَةَ» وأما قوله عليه الصلاة والسلام:\n«فِرَّ مِنَ المَجْذُومِ فِرَارَكَ مِنَ الأَسَدِ» ، وقوله: «لاَ يورد مُمْرِضٌ عَلَى مُصِحٍّ» ، فهو محمول على حسم المادة، وسد الذريعة لئلا يحدث للمخالط شيء من ذلك، فيظنه بسبب المخالطة، فيثبت العدوى التي نفاها الشارع، هذا المختار في الجمع بين الحديثين. والله تعالى أعلم. وإنما أطلت في المسألة لِمَسْ الحاجة لأن التأليف وقع في زمن الوباء، حفظنا الله من وبالها.\nوقيل: إن الذين خرجوا من ديارهم قوم من بني إسرائيل، أُمروا بالجهاد، فخافوا الموت بالقتل في الجهاد، فخرجوا من ديارهم فراراً من ذلك، فأماتهم الله ليعرفهم أنهم لا ينجيهم من الموت شيء، ثم أحياهم وأمرهم بالجهاد، بقوله: وَقاتِلُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ الآية. وقوله تعالى: إِنَّ اللَّهَ لَذُو فَضْلٍ عَلَى النَّاسِ حيث أنزل بهم رحمته، ففروا منها، ولم يعاقبهم، حيث أحياهم بعد موتهم، وَلكِنَّ أَكْثَرَ النَّاسِ لا يَشْكُرُونَ إذ لا يفهم النعم في طي النقم إلا القليل، فيشكروا الله في السراء والضراء.\nالإشارة: ألم ترَ أيها السامع إلى الذين خرجوا من ديار عوائدهم وأوطان شهواتهم، وهم جماعة أهل التجريد، القاصدين إلى صفاء التوحيد، والغرق في بحر التفريد، حذراً من موت أرواحهم بالجهل والفَرْقِ، فاصطفاهم الله لحضرته، وجذبهم إلى مشاهدة ذاته، فقال لهم الله: موتوا عن حظوظكم، وغيبوا عن وجودكم، فلما ماتوا عن حظوظهم، وغابوا عن وجودهم، أحياهم الله بالعلم والمعرفة، إِنَّ اللَّهَ لَذُو فَضْلٍ عَلَى النَّاسِ حيث فتح لهم باب السلوك، وهيأهم لمعرفة ملك الملوك، وَلكِنَّ أَكْثَرَ النَّاسِ لا يَشْكُرُونَ حيث تجلّى لهم، وعرَّفهم به، وهم لا يشعرون، إلا من فتح الله بصيرتهم، وقليل ما هم.\nثم حرّض الحق تعالى المؤمنين على الجهاد، فقال:","quran_ar":"أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى الَّذِينَ خَرَجُوا مِنْ دِيارِهِمْ وَهُمْ أُلُوفٌ حَذَرَ الْمَوْتِ فَقالَ لَهُمُ اللَّهُ مُوتُوا ثُمَّ أَحْياهُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ لَذُو فَضْلٍ عَلَى النَّاسِ وَلكِنَّ أَكْثَرَ النَّاسِ لا يَشْكُرُونَ (243)","quran_en":"243. Have you not considered those who left their homes in their thousands, fearing death? God said to them, “Die”; then He brought them to life. God is indeed bountiful towards people, but most people do not give thanks.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"A question that awakens wonder","html":"<p>I say: the question expresses wonder and awakens interest. The seeing is of the heart (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">رؤية قلبية<\/bdi>); the <i>wāw<\/i> introduces a circumstance (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">واو الحال<\/bdi>); and “fearing” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حذر<\/bdi>) is an accusative of purpose (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول من أجله<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the question expresses wonder and awakens interest. The seeing is of the heart ( رؤية قلبية ); the wāw introduces a circumstance ( واو الحال ); and “fearing” ( حذر ) is an accusative of purpose ( مفعول من أجله )."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Those who fled the plague","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: have you not looked, Muḥammad, with the eye of reflection and consideration, at those who left their homes in their thousands—ten, thirty, forty or seventy thousand—fearing death at a time of plague?<\/p><p>They were in a village called Dāwardān. When plague struck, one group fled and another remained. Most of those who remained perished, while those who left escaped. The latter returned, and the survivors said: “Had we done as they did, we would have remained alive. If plague strikes us a second time, we shall leave.” It struck the following year. They all fled and descended into a broad valley. An angel called from its lower end, and another from its upper end:<\/p><p>“Die!” They all died. Eight days or more passed until they swelled; it is also said that they became bones. God’s prophet Ḥizqīl passed them, prayed to God Most High and interceded for them. God brought them to life. They lived for a long time with the mark of death upon them: any garment they wore became like a shroud. This continued among their descendants.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-190\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B183]<\/a><\/p><p>Al-Aṣmaʿī said: when plague struck Basra, a man left on a donkey with his family and a slave driving the donkey. The slave began to recite:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">God cannot be outstripped upon a donkey,<br>nor upon a flying, shaggy-maned steed;<br>God may already stand before the night-traveller.<\/blockquote><p>The man returned with his family.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: have you not looked, Muḥammad, with the eye of reflection and consideration, at those who left their homes in their thousands—ten, thirty, forty or seventy thousand—fearing death at a time of plague? They were in a village called Dāwardān. When plague struck, one group fled and another remained. Most of those who remained perished, while those who left escaped. The latter returned, and the survivors said: “Had we done as they did, we would have remained alive. If plague strikes us a second time, we shall leave.” It struck the following year. They all fled and descended into a broad valley. An angel called from its lower end, and another from its upper end: “Die!” They all died. Eight days or more passed until they swelled; it is also said that they became bones. God’s prophet Ḥizqīl passed them, prayed to God Most High and interceded for them. God brought them to life. They lived for a long time with the mark of death upon them: any garment they wore became like a shroud. This continued among their descendants. [B183] Al-Aṣmaʿī said: when plague struck Basra, a man left on a donkey with his family and a slave driving the donkey. The slave began to recite: God cannot be outstripped upon a donkey, nor upon a flying, shaggy-maned steed; God may already stand before the night-traveller. The man returned with his family."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The author’s discussion of flight, entry and contagion","html":"<p>The verse indicates that flight from plague was forbidden in that dispensation, as it is forbidden in ours. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf related that the Prophet said: “When you hear of this epidemic in a land, do not enter it; when it occurs in a land while you are there, do not leave it.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-191\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B184]<\/a><\/p><p>I say: the imams disagreed over flight and entry. Some regarded prohibition in both as the recognised position, holding to the apparent meaning of the hadith; others regarded disapproval as the recognised position. The preferred ruling for flight is prohibition. For entry, a distinction is made: someone whose certainty is strong and whose affirmation of unity is pure may enter; someone whose certainty is weak, so that if something befalls him he ascribes efficacy to other than God, may not enter.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-192\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B184]<\/a><\/p><p>In ʿĀʾisha’s report, she says: “I asked, ‘Messenger of God, what is plague?’ He said: ‘A swelling like the swelling of a camel. One who remains in it is like a martyr, and one who flees from it is like one who flees the battle line.’” Ibn Ḥajar said: the person who remains receives a martyr’s reward only on condition that he knows nothing can befall him except what God has decreed for him, entrusts his affairs to Him and is content with His decree, and remains in his place without leaving in flight. When the person who stays fulfils these conditions, he receives the reward of martyrdom.<\/p><p>This includes three cases. First, one who possesses these qualities, contracts plague and dies: he is a martyr. Second, one who contracts it but does not die of it: he is a martyr, even if he dies afterwards. Third, one who never contracts it and dies of something else, sooner or later: he is a martyr if these three conditions are fulfilled. One who lacks the three conditions is not a martyr, even if he dies of plague. And God knows best. Here his words end.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-193\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B185]<\/a><\/p><p>As for coming from a plague-stricken land to one free from it, it is permissible, and the person is not prevented from entering. Al-Bājī, Ibn Ḥajar, al-Ḥaṭṭāb and others said this, on the basis of the Prophet’s words: “There is no contagion and no evil omen.” As for his words,<\/p><p>“Flee from the leper as you flee from a lion,” and “Do not bring sick animals among healthy ones,” these are understood as cutting off the occasion and blocking the means (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سد الذريعة<\/bdi>), lest something similar occur to the person who mixes with the sick, and he suppose that it was caused by the mixing, thus affirming the contagion negated by the Lawgiver. This is the preferred reconciliation of the two reports. And God Most High knows best. I have prolonged this discussion only because the need is pressing: this work was composed at a time of epidemic. May God preserve us from its harm.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-194\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B184]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The verse indicates that flight from plague was forbidden in that dispensation, as it is forbidden in ours. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf related that the Prophet said: “When you hear of this epidemic in a land, do not enter it; when it occurs in a land while you are there, do not leave it.” [B184] I say: the imams disagreed over flight and entry. Some regarded prohibition in both as the recognised position, holding to the apparent meaning of the hadith; others regarded disapproval as the recognised position. The preferred ruling for flight is prohibition. For entry, a distinction is made: someone whose certainty is strong and whose affirmation of unity is pure may enter; someone whose certainty is weak, so that if something befalls him he ascribes efficacy to other than God, may not enter. [B184] In ʿĀʾisha’s report, she says: “I asked, ‘Messenger of God, what is plague?’ He said: ‘A swelling like the swelling of a camel. One who remains in it is like a martyr, and one who flees from it is like one who flees the battle line.’” Ibn Ḥajar said: the person who remains receives a martyr’s reward only on condition that he knows nothing can befall him except what God has decreed for him, entrusts his affairs to Him and is content with His decree, and remains in his place without leaving in flight. When the person who stays fulfils these conditions, he receives the reward of martyrdom. This includes three cases. First, one who possesses these qualities, contracts plague and dies: he is a martyr. Second, one who contracts it but does not die of it: he is a martyr, even if he dies afterwards. Third, one who never contracts it and dies of something else, sooner or later: he is a martyr if these three conditions are fulfilled. One who lacks the three conditions is not a martyr, even if he dies of plague. And God knows best. Here his words end. [B185] As for coming from a plague-stricken land to one free from it, it is permissible, and the person is not prevented from entering. Al-Bājī, Ibn Ḥajar, al-Ḥaṭṭāb and others said this, on the basis of the Prophet’s words: “There is no contagion and no evil omen.” As for his words, “Flee from the leper as you flee from a lion,” and “Do not bring sick animals among healthy ones,” these are understood as cutting off the occasion and blocking the means ( سد الذريعة ), lest something similar occur to the person who mixes with the sick, and he suppose that it was caused by the mixing, thus affirming the contagion negated by the Lawgiver. This is the preferred reconciliation of the two reports. And God Most High knows best. I have prolonged this discussion only because the need is pressing: this work was composed at a time of epidemic. May God preserve us from its harm. [B184]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The alternative account and gratitude amid affliction","html":"<p>It is also said that those who left their homes were Israelites commanded to fight. Fearing death in battle, they left their homes to escape it. God caused them to die so that they would know nothing could save them from death, then brought them to life and commanded them to fight in His words: “Fight in the path of God.”<\/p><p>“God is indeed bountiful towards people”: He sent His mercy upon them, and they fled from it, yet He did not punish them, but brought them back to life after death. “But most people do not give thanks”: only a few understand the blessings folded within afflictions and therefore thank God in both ease and adversity.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"It is also said that those who left their homes were Israelites commanded to fight. Fearing death in battle, they left their homes to escape it. God caused them to die so that they would know nothing could save them from death, then brought them to life and commanded them to fight in His words: “Fight in the path of God.” “God is indeed bountiful towards people”: He sent His mercy upon them, and they fled from it, yet He did not punish them, but brought them back to life after death. “But most people do not give thanks”: only a few understand the blessings folded within afflictions and therefore thank God in both ease and adversity."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Death to personal interests, life through gnosis","html":"<p>Have you not considered, listener, those who left the homes of their habits and the homelands of their desires? They are the company of those stripped of attachments (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل التجريد<\/bdi>), seeking purity of the affirmation of unity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوحيد<\/bdi>) and immersion in the sea of singleness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التفريد<\/bdi>), fearing that their spirits might die through ignorance and separation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفرق<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>God chose them for His presence and drew them to witnessing His Essence. He said to them: “Die to your personal interests and become absent from your existence.” When they died to their interests and became absent from their existence, God brought them to life through knowledge and gnosis. God is indeed bountiful towards people: He opened for them the door of wayfaring and prepared them to know the King of kings. But most people do not give thanks. He discloses Himself to them and makes Himself known, while they are unaware—except those whose inward sight God opens, and few they are.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Have you not considered, listener, those who left the homes of their habits and the homelands of their desires? They are the company of those stripped of attachments ( أهل التجريد ), seeking purity of the affirmation of unity ( التوحيد ) and immersion in the sea of singleness ( التفريد ), fearing that their spirits might die through ignorance and separation ( الفرق ). God chose them for His presence and drew them to witnessing His Essence. He said to them: “Die to your personal interests and become absent from your existence.” When they died to their interests and became absent from their existence, God brought them to life through knowledge and gnosis. God is indeed bountiful towards people: He opened for them the door of wayfaring and prepared them to know the King of kings. But most people do not give thanks. He discloses Himself to them and makes Himself known, while they are unaware—except those whose inward sight God opens, and few they are."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then urges the believers to struggle in His cause.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then urges the believers to struggle in His cause."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C268.1","page":268,"ar":"الأفيح والفياح: كل موضع واسع، ومنه: روضة فيحاء.","en":"Afyaḥ and fiyāḥ: any spacious place; hence the expression rawḍa fayḥāʾ, a spacious meadow.","anchor":"وادياً أفيح"},{"id":"C269.1","page":269,"ar":"ذكر القرطبي البيت الثاني كاملاً، وهو: أو يأتي الحتف على مقدار ... قد يصبح الله أمام الساري.","en":"Al-Qurṭubī gives the second verse in full: “Or death comes at an appointed measure; God may be before the night traveller.”","anchor":"قد يصبح الله أمَامَ السارِي"}],"english_characters":6002,"arabic_characters":4126},{"id":"q2-244","q":"2:244","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:244 · Striving in God’s path","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 270–271","cairo":"I:270–271","source_page_first":270,"source_page_last":271,"ar":"وَقاتِلُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ سَمِيعٌ عَلِيمٌ (244)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَقاتِلُوا الكفار فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ وإعلاء كلمة الله حتى يكون الدين كله لله، وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ سَمِيعٌ لأقوالكم ودعائكم، عَلِيمٌ بنياتكم وإخلاصكم، فيجازي المخلصين، ويحرم المخلطين.\nالإشارة: وجاهدوا نفوسكم في طريق الوصول إلى الله، وأديموا السير إلى حضرة الله، فحضرة القدوس محرمة على أهل النفوس. قال الششتري:\nإنْ تُرِدَ وَصْلَنَا فَمَوْتكَ شَرْطٌ ... لا يَنَالُ الوِصَالَ مَنْ فِيه فَضْلَه\nومجاهدة النفس هو تحميلها ما يثقل عليها، وبُعدها مما يخف عليها، حتى لا يثقل عليها شيء، ولا تشره إلى شيء، بل يكون هواها ما يقضيه عليها مولاها. قيل لبعضهم: [ما تشتهي؟ قال: ما يقضي الله] . واعلموا أيها السائرون أن الله سميع لأذكاركم، عليم بإخلاصكم ومقاصدكم.\nولما كان الجهاد يحتاج إلى مؤنة التجهيز، وليس كل الناس يقدر على ذلك، رَغَّبَ الحق تعالى الأقوياء بالإنفاق على الفقراء، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَقاتِلُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّ اللَّهَ سَمِيعٌ عَلِيمٌ (244)","quran_en":"244. Fight in the path of God, and know that God is Hearing, Knowing.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The intention of struggle","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: fight the unbelievers in God’s path, raising God’s word, until religion belongs wholly to God. Know that God hears your words and prayers and knows your intentions and sincerity. He recompenses the sincere and deprives those whose motives are mixed.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: fight the unbelievers in God’s path, raising God’s word, until religion belongs wholly to God. Know that God hears your words and prayers and knows your intentions and sincerity. He recompenses the sincere and deprives those whose motives are mixed."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Training the self until it accepts its Lord’s decree","html":"<p>Struggle against your selves on the path of reaching God, and persist in travelling towards His presence, for the presence of the All-Holy is forbidden to those still possessed by their selves. Al-Shushtarī said:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">If you desire union with us, your death is a condition;<br>one in whom a remnant remains does not attain union.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-195\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B186]<\/a><\/blockquote><p>Struggling against the self means making it bear what it finds burdensome and keeping it from what it finds easy, until nothing burdens it and it eagerly desires nothing. Its desire then becomes whatever its Master decrees for it. Someone was asked: “What do you desire?” He replied: “Whatever God decrees.” Know, travellers, that God hears your remembrances and knows your sincerity and purposes.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Struggle against your selves on the path of reaching God, and persist in travelling towards His presence, for the presence of the All-Holy is forbidden to those still possessed by their selves. Al-Shushtarī said: If you desire union with us, your death is a condition; one in whom a remnant remains does not attain union. [B186] Struggling against the self means making it bear what it finds burdensome and keeping it from what it finds easy, until nothing burdens it and it eagerly desires nothing. Its desire then becomes whatever its Master decrees for it. Someone was asked: “What do you desire?” He replied: “Whatever God decrees.” Know, travellers, that God hears your remembrances and knows your sincerity and purposes."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Since fighting requires the expense of preparation, and not everyone can afford it, the Real Most High encourages those with means to spend upon the poor.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Since fighting requires the expense of preparation, and not everyone can afford it, the Real Most High encourages those with means to spend upon the poor."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1170,"arabic_characters":874},{"id":"q2-245","q":"2:245","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:245 · The goodly loan, contraction and expansion","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 271–273","cairo":"I:271–273","source_page_first":271,"source_page_last":273,"ar":"مَنْ ذَا الَّذِي يُقْرِضُ اللَّهَ قَرْضاً حَسَناً فَيُضاعِفَهُ لَهُ أَضْعافاً كَثِيرَةً وَاللَّهُ يَقْبِضُ وَيَبْصُطُ وَإِلَيْهِ تُرْجَعُونَ (245)\nقلت: القرض هو القطع، أطْلِقَ على السلف لأن المُقْرِضَ يقطع قطعة من ماله ويدفعها للمستلف، والمراد بها الصدقة لأن المتصدق يدفع الصدقة فيردها الحق تعالى له بضعف أمثالها فأشبهت القرض في مطلق الرد.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: مَنْ هذا الذي يعامل الله تعالى ويقرضه قَرْضاً حَسَناً بأن يتصدق على عباده صدقة حسنة بنية خالصة، فيُكثرها الله تعالى له أَضْعافاً كَثِيرَةً بسبعمائة إلى ما لا نهاية له، ولا يحمله خوف الفقر على ترك الصدقة فإنَّ الله تعالى يقبض الرزق عمن يشاء ولو قل إعطاؤه، ويبسط الرزق على من يشاء ولو كثر إعطاؤه، بل يقبض على من قبض يده شحّاً وبخلاً، ويبسط على من بسط يده عطاءً وبذلاً، يقول: «يا ابن آدم أنفق أنفق عليك» ، «أُنْفِقْ ولا تَخْش من ذي العرش إقلالا» .\nونسبة القرض إليه تعالى ترغيب وتقريب للإفهام، كما قال في الحديث القدسي: «يقول اللهُ تعالى يوم القيامة: يَا ابْنَ آدَمَ، مَرِضْتُ فَلَمْ تَعُدْني، قَالَ: يَا رَبّ! كَيْفَ أَعُودُكَ وأنت رب العالمين؟ قال: أَمَا عَلِمْتَ أَنَّ عَبْدِي فُلاَناً مَرِضَ فَلَمْ تَعُدْهُ. أَمَا إِنَكَ لَوْ عُدْتَهُ لوجدتنى عنده. يَا ابْنَ آدَم اسْتَطْعَمتُكَ فَلَمْ تُطْعِمني. قال:\nيَا رَبّ! كَيْفَ أُطْعِمُكَ وَأَنْتَ رب العالمين؟ قال: أما علمت أَنَّهُ اسْتَطْعَمَكَ عَبْدِي فُلاَنٌ فَلَمْ تُطْعمْه؟ أَمَا عَلِمْتَ أَنَّكَ لَوْ أَطْعَمْتَه لَوَجَدْتَ ذَلكَ عِنْدِي. يَا ابْنَ آدم استسقيتك فلم تسقنى. قال: يا رب كيف أَسْقِيك وَأَنْتَ رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ؟\nقال: اسْتَسْقَاكَ عَبْدِي فُلاَنٌ فَلَمْ تسْقِه. أَمَا عَلِمْتَ أَنَّكَ لَوْ سَقَيْتَهُ لوَجَدْتَ ذَلِكَ عِنْدِي» .\nالإشارة: من هذا الذي يقطع قلبه عن حب الدارين، ويرفع همته عن الكونين، فإن الله (يضاعفه له أضعافاً كثيرة) بأن يُمَلِّكَهُ الوجودَ بما فيه، «أنت مع الأكوان ما لم تشهد المكون، فإذا شهدت المكون كانت الأكوان\nمعك» ، (والله يقبض ويبسط) فيقبض الوجود تحت حكمك وهمتك، إن رفعت همتك عنه، ويبسط يدك بالتصرف فيه، إن علقت همتك بخالقه. أو يقبض القلوب بالفقد والوحشة، ويبسطها بالإيناس والبهجة. أو يقبض الأرواح بالوفاة، ويبسطها بالحياة. والقبض والبسط عند أهل التصوف: حالتان تتعاقبان على القلوب تعاقب الليل والنهار، فإذا غلب حال الخوف كان مقبوضا، وإذا غلب حال الرجاء كان مبسوطاً، وهذا حال السائرين. أما الواصلون فقد اعتدل خوفهم ورجاؤهم، فلا يؤثر فيهم قبض ولا بسط، لأنهم مالكو الأحوالَ.\nقال القشيري: فإذا كاشف العبدَ بنعت جماله بسطَه، وإذا كاشفه بنعت جلاله قَبضه. فالقبض يوجب إيحاشَه، والبسط يُوجب إيناسه، واعلم أنه يَرُدُّ العبد إلى حال بشريته، فيقبضه حتى لا يُطيق ذَرَّة، ويأخذه مَرَّة عن نعوته، فيجد لحمل ما يَرِدُ عليه قدرة وطاقة، قال الشّبلى رضي الله عنه: (من عَرَفَ الله حمَل السموات والأرض على شَعرة من جَفْن عينه، ومن لم يعرف الله- جلّ وعلا- لو تعلق به جَناح بعوضة لضجَّ) .\nوقال أهل المعرفة: [إذا قَبض قَبض حتى لا طاقة، وإذا بسط بسط حتى لا فاقة، والكل منه وإليه] . ومن عرف أن الله هو القابض الباسط، لم يَعتِب أحداً من الخلق، ولا يسكن إليه في إقبال ولا إدبار، ولم ييأس منه في البلاء، ولا يسكن إليه في عطاء، فلا يكون له تدبير أبداً. هـ.\nولكلٍّ من القبض والبسط آداب، فآداب القبض: السكون تحت مجاري الأقدار، وانتظار الفرج من الكريم الغفار.\nوآداب البسط: كف اللسان، وقبض العنان، والحياء من الكريم المنان. والبسط مَزَلَّة أقدام الرجال. قال بعضهم: (فُتح عليَّ بابٌ من البسط فزللتُ زَلَّة، فحُجِبْتُ عن مقامي ثلاثين سنة) . ولذلك قيل: قف على البساط وإياك والانبساطَ واعلم أن القبض والبسط فوق الخوف والرجاء، وفوق القبض والبسط: الهيبة والأنس فالخوف والرجاء للمؤمنين، والقبض والبسط للسائرين، والهيبة والأنس للعارفين، ثم المحو في وجود العين للمتمكنين، فلا هيبة لهم، ولا أنس، ولا علم، ولا حس. وأنشدوا:\nفلو كُنْتَ مِنْ أَهلِ الوُجودِ حقيقةً ... لغبْتَ عَن الأكوانِ والعرشِ والكرسي\nوكُنْتَ بلا حالٍ مَع اللهِ واقفاً ... تُصان عَنْ التّذْكَارِ للجِن والإنس(1)\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى قصة من أمر بالجهاد فجبن عنه، ترهيبا من التشبه به، فقال:","quran_ar":"مَنْ ذَا الَّذِي يُقْرِضُ اللَّهَ قَرْضاً حَسَناً فَيُضاعِفَهُ لَهُ أَضْعافاً كَثِيرَةً وَاللَّهُ يَقْبِضُ وَيَبْصُطُ وَإِلَيْهِ تُرْجَعُونَ (245)","quran_en":"245. Who is it that will lend God a goodly loan, so that He may multiply it for him many times over? God contracts and expands, and to Him you will be returned.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Why charity is called a loan","html":"<p>I say: <i>qarḍ<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القرض<\/bdi>) means cutting. It is applied to a loan because the lender cuts off a portion of his property and gives it to the borrower. Here it means charity: the donor gives it, and the Real Most High returns it to him multiplied many times. It thus resembles a loan in the general respect of being returned.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: qarḍ ( القرض ) means cutting. It is applied to a loan because the lender cuts off a portion of his property and gives it to the borrower. Here it means charity: the donor gives it, and the Real Most High returns it to him multiplied many times. It thus resembles a loan in the general respect of being returned."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Giving to God through giving to His servants","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: who will deal with God Most High and lend Him a goodly loan by giving His servants a good charity with a sincere intention? God will multiply it for him many times: seven hundredfold and beyond, without end. Fear of poverty must not make him abandon charity. God restricts provision for whom He wills, even if that person gives little, and expands provision for whom He wills, even if he gives much. Rather, He restricts the one who withholds his hand through avarice and miserliness, and expands the one who opens his hand in giving and generosity. He says: “Son of Adam, spend, and I shall spend upon you”; and, “Spend, and do not fear impoverishment from the Possessor of the Throne.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-196\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B187]<\/a><\/p><p>Attributing the loan to God Most High encourages giving and brings the meaning within reach of understanding. Thus the sacred report (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حديث قدسي<\/bdi>) says:<\/p><p>“God Most High will say on the Day of Resurrection: ‘Son of Adam, I was ill and you did not visit Me.’ He will reply: ‘Lord, how could I visit You when You are Lord of the worlds?’ He will say: ‘Did you not know that My servant so-and-so was ill, and you did not visit him? Had you visited him, you would have found Me with him. Son of Adam, I asked you for food and you did not feed Me.’ He will reply: ‘Lord, how could I feed You when You are Lord of the worlds?’ He will say: ‘Did you not know that My servant so-and-so asked you for food, and you did not feed him? Had you fed him, you would have found that with Me. Son of Adam, I asked you for drink and you did not give Me drink.’ He will reply: ‘Lord, how could I give You drink when You are Lord of the worlds?’ He will say: ‘My servant so-and-so asked you for drink, and you did not give him drink. Had you given him drink, you would have found that with Me.’”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-197\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B187]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: who will deal with God Most High and lend Him a goodly loan by giving His servants a good charity with a sincere intention? God will multiply it for him many times: seven hundredfold and beyond, without end. Fear of poverty must not make him abandon charity. God restricts provision for whom He wills, even if that person gives little, and expands provision for whom He wills, even if he gives much. Rather, He restricts the one who withholds his hand through avarice and miserliness, and expands the one who opens his hand in giving and generosity. He says: “Son of Adam, spend, and I shall spend upon you”; and, “Spend, and do not fear impoverishment from the Possessor of the Throne.” [B187] Attributing the loan to God Most High encourages giving and brings the meaning within reach of understanding. Thus the sacred report ( حديث قدسي ) says: “God Most High will say on the Day of Resurrection: ‘Son of Adam, I was ill and you did not visit Me.’ He will reply: ‘Lord, how could I visit You when You are Lord of the worlds?’ He will say: ‘Did you not know that My servant so-and-so was ill, and you did not visit him? Had you visited him, you would have found Me with him. Son of Adam, I asked you for food and you did not feed Me.’ He will reply: ‘Lord, how could I feed You when You are Lord of the worlds?’ He will say: ‘Did you not know that My servant so-and-so asked you for food, and you did not feed him? Had you fed him, you would have found that with Me. Son of Adam, I asked you for drink and you did not give Me drink.’ He will reply: ‘Lord, how could I give You drink when You are Lord of the worlds?’ He will say: ‘My servant so-and-so asked you for drink, and you did not give him drink. Had you given him drink, you would have found that with Me.’” [B187]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Raising aspiration beyond the two worlds","html":"<p>Who will cut his heart free from love of the two abodes and raise his aspiration beyond the two worlds? God will multiply this for him many times by granting him mastery over existence and all it contains. “You are with created things so long as you do not witness their Maker. When you witness their Maker, created things<\/p><p>are with you.”<\/p><p>“God contracts and expands”: He gathers existence beneath your judgment and aspiration if you raise your aspiration beyond it, and extends your hand to exercise disposal within it if you attach your aspiration to its Creator. Or He contracts hearts through loss and loneliness, and expands them through intimacy and joy. Or He contracts spirits through death and expands them through life.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Who will cut his heart free from love of the two abodes and raise his aspiration beyond the two worlds? God will multiply this for him many times by granting him mastery over existence and all it contains. “You are with created things so long as you do not witness their Maker. When you witness their Maker, created things are with you.” “God contracts and expands”: He gathers existence beneath your judgment and aspiration if you raise your aspiration beyond it, and extends your hand to exercise disposal within it if you attach your aspiration to its Creator. Or He contracts hearts through loss and loneliness, and expands them through intimacy and joy. Or He contracts spirits through death and expands them through life."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The states of contraction and expansion","html":"<p>Among the people of Sufism, contraction and expansion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القبض والبسط<\/bdi>) are two states that alternate upon hearts as night and day alternate. When fear predominates, the heart is contracted; when hope predominates, it is expanded. This is the state of travellers. As for those who have arrived, their fear and hope are balanced. Neither contraction nor expansion affects them, for they have become masters of states.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-198\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B188]<\/a><\/p><p>Al-Qushayrī said: when He unveils Himself to the servant through the quality of His beauty, He expands him; when He unveils Himself through the quality of His majesty, He contracts him. Contraction brings loneliness, and expansion brings intimacy. Know that He returns the servant to his human condition and contracts him until he cannot bear a speck; at another time He takes him away from his own attributes, and he finds power and capacity to bear whatever comes upon him.<\/p><p>Al-Shiblī said: “One who knows God can carry the heavens and earth upon a hair of his eyelid. One who does not know God—majestic and exalted is He—would cry out if the wing of a gnat were attached to him.”<\/p><p>The people of gnosis said: “When He contracts, He contracts until no capacity remains; when He expands, He expands until no need remains. All is from Him and to Him.” Whoever knows that God is the Contractor and Expander (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القابض الباسط<\/bdi>) reproaches no creature and rests in none, whether approaching or withdrawing. He does not despair of Him in affliction or settle into complacency with Him in a gift; he therefore never engages in self-directed planning. Here the quotation ends.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Among the people of Sufism, contraction and expansion ( القبض والبسط ) are two states that alternate upon hearts as night and day alternate. When fear predominates, the heart is contracted; when hope predominates, it is expanded. This is the state of travellers. As for those who have arrived, their fear and hope are balanced. Neither contraction nor expansion affects them, for they have become masters of states. [B188] Al-Qushayrī said: when He unveils Himself to the servant through the quality of His beauty, He expands him; when He unveils Himself through the quality of His majesty, He contracts him. Contraction brings loneliness, and expansion brings intimacy. Know that He returns the servant to his human condition and contracts him until he cannot bear a speck; at another time He takes him away from his own attributes, and he finds power and capacity to bear whatever comes upon him. Al-Shiblī said: “One who knows God can carry the heavens and earth upon a hair of his eyelid. One who does not know God—majestic and exalted is He—would cry out if the wing of a gnat were attached to him.” The people of gnosis said: “When He contracts, He contracts until no capacity remains; when He expands, He expands until no need remains. All is from Him and to Him.” Whoever knows that God is the Contractor and Expander ( القابض الباسط ) reproaches no creature and rests in none, whether approaching or withdrawing. He does not despair of Him in affliction or settle into complacency with Him in a gift; he therefore never engages in self-directed planning. Here the quotation ends."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The proper conduct of each state","html":"<p>Each of contraction and expansion has its proper manners (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">آداب<\/bdi>). The manners of contraction are stillness beneath the courses of the decrees and awaiting relief from the Generous, the Forgiving. The manners of expansion are restraining the tongue, drawing in the reins and having modesty before the Generous Bestower. Expansion is a place where men’s feet slip. One of them said: “A door of expansion opened to me. I slipped once and was veiled from my station for thirty years.” Hence the saying: “Stand upon the carpet (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البساط<\/bdi>), but beware of overfamiliarity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الانبساط<\/bdi>).”<\/p><p>Know that contraction and expansion are above fear and hope; above contraction and expansion are awe and intimacy (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الهيبة والأنس<\/bdi>). Fear and hope belong to believers; contraction and expansion to travellers; awe and intimacy to gnostics. Then comes effacement within the existence of the very Reality (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المحو في وجود العين<\/bdi>), for those firmly established. They have neither awe nor intimacy, neither knowledge nor sensation. They recited:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Had you truly been among the people of Being,<br>you would have been absent from created things, the Throne and the Footstool;<br>you would have stood with God, without a state,<br>preserved from recollection of jinn and humankind.<\/blockquote>","authorial":false,"text":"Each of contraction and expansion has its proper manners ( آداب ). The manners of contraction are stillness beneath the courses of the decrees and awaiting relief from the Generous, the Forgiving. The manners of expansion are restraining the tongue, drawing in the reins and having modesty before the Generous Bestower. Expansion is a place where men’s feet slip. One of them said: “A door of expansion opened to me. I slipped once and was veiled from my station for thirty years.” Hence the saying: “Stand upon the carpet ( البساط ), but beware of overfamiliarity ( الانبساط ).” Know that contraction and expansion are above fear and hope; above contraction and expansion are awe and intimacy ( الهيبة والأنس ). Fear and hope belong to believers; contraction and expansion to travellers; awe and intimacy to gnostics. Then comes effacement within the existence of the very Reality ( المحو في وجود العين ), for those firmly established. They have neither awe nor intimacy, neither knowledge nor sensation. They recited: Had you truly been among the people of Being, you would have been absent from created things, the Throne and the Footstool; you would have stood with God, without a state, preserved from recollection of jinn and humankind."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then relates the story of people commanded to fight who shrank from it, warning against resembling them.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then relates the story of people commanded to fight who shrank from it, warning against resembling them."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C272.1","page":272,"ar":"ورد هذان البيتان في قصة مع أبي سعيد الخراز، ذكرها القشيري في الرسالة.","en":"These two verses occur in a story involving Abū Saʿīd al-Kharrāz, which al-Qushayrī mentions in the Risāla.","anchor":"تُصان عَنْ التّذْكَارِ للجِن والإنس"}],"english_characters":5804,"arabic_characters":3654},{"id":"q2-246","q":"2:246","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:246 · A request to fight and the test of resolve","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 273–274","cairo":"I:273–274","source_page_first":273,"source_page_last":274,"ar":"أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى الْمَلَإِ مِنْ بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ مِنْ بَعْدِ مُوسى إِذْ قالُوا لِنَبِيٍّ لَهُمُ ابْعَثْ لَنا مَلِكاً نُقاتِلْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ قالَ هَلْ عَسَيْتُمْ إِنْ كُتِبَ عَلَيْكُمُ الْقِتالُ أَلاَّ تُقاتِلُوا قالُوا وَما لَنا أَلاَّ نُقاتِلَ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ وَقَدْ أُخْرِجْنا مِنْ دِيارِنا وَأَبْنائِنا فَلَمَّا كُتِبَ عَلَيْهِمُ الْقِتالُ تَوَلَّوْا إِلاَّ قَلِيلاً مِنْهُمْ وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ بِالظَّالِمِينَ (246)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: أَلَمْ تَرَ يا محمد- فتعتبر- إِلَى قصة جماعة مِنْ بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ مِنْ بَعْدِ موت مُوسى حين طلبوا الجهاد، وقالوا لِنَبِيٍّ لَهُمُ يقال له: شمويل، وقيل: شمعون: ابْعَثْ لَنا مَلِكاً يَسوس أمرنا ونرجع إليه في رأينا إذ الحرب لا تستقيم بغير إمام نُقاتِلْ معه فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ، قالَ لهم ذلك النبيّ: هَلْ عَسَيْتُمْ إِنْ كُتِبَ عَلَيْكُمُ الْقِتالُ أَلَّا تُقاتِلُوا أي: هل أنتم قريب من التولي والفرار إن كُتب عليكم القتال؟ والمعنى: أتوقع جُبْنكم عن القتال إنْ فُرض عليكم. والأصل: عساكم أن تجبنوا إن فرض عليكم، فأدخل (هل) على فعل التوقع، مستفهماً عما هو المتوقع عنده، تقريراً وتثبيتاً.\nقالُوا في جوابه: وَما لَنا أَلَّا نُقاتِلَ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ أيْ: أيُّ مانع يمنعنا من القتال وقد وُجد داعيه؟ وهو تسلط العدو علينا فأخْرَجَنَا من ديارنا وأسَرَ أبناءَنا، وكان الله تعالى سلط عليهم جالوت ومن معه من العمالقة، كانوا يسكنون ساحل بحر الروم(1) بين مصر وفلسطين، وذلك لمَّا عصوا وسفكوا الدماء، فخرَّبَ بيت المقدس، وحرق التوراة، وأخذ التابوت الذي كانوا ينتصرون به، وسبي نساءهم وذراريهم(2) . رُوِيَ أنه سبي من أبناء ملوكهم أربعمائة وأربعين، فسألوا نبيهم أن يبعث لهم ملكاً يجاهدون معه، فَلَمَّا كُتِبَ عَلَيْهِمُ الْقِتالُ ويَسر لهم ملكاً يسوسهم وهو طالوت، جبنُوا وتولوا إِلَّا قَلِيلًا مِنْهُمْ، وهم من عَبَرَ النهر مع طالوت، وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ بِالظَّالِمِينَ فيخزيهم ويُفسد رأيهم. نعوذ بالله من ذلك.\nالإشارة: ترى كثيراً من الناس يتمنون أن لو ظفروا بشيخ التربية، ويقولون: لو وجدناه لجاهدنا أنفسنا أكثر من غيرنا، فلما ظهر، وعُرف بالتربية، تولى ونكص على عقبيه، وتعلل بالإنكار وعدم الأهلية، إلا قليلاً ممن خصه الله بعنايته (وَاللَّهُ يَخْتَصُّ بِرحمَتِهِ مَن يَشَاءُ) . (واللَّهُ ذو الفضل العظيم) . سبحان مَن لم يجعل الدليل على أوليائه إلا من حيث الدليل عليه، ولم يوصل إليهم إلا مَن أراد أن يوصله إليه.\nثم عيّن لهم الملك الذي طلبوا، فقال:","quran_ar":"أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى الْمَلَإِ مِنْ بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ مِنْ بَعْدِ مُوسى إِذْ قالُوا لِنَبِيٍّ لَهُمُ ابْعَثْ لَنا مَلِكاً نُقاتِلْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ قالَ هَلْ عَسَيْتُمْ إِنْ كُتِبَ عَلَيْكُمُ الْقِتالُ أَلاَّ تُقاتِلُوا قالُوا وَما لَنا أَلاَّ نُقاتِلَ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ وَقَدْ أُخْرِجْنا مِنْ دِيارِنا وَأَبْنائِنا فَلَمَّا كُتِبَ عَلَيْهِمُ الْقِتالُ تَوَلَّوْا إِلاَّ قَلِيلاً مِنْهُمْ وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ بِالظَّالِمِينَ (246)","quran_en":"246. Have you not considered the leaders of the Children of Israel after Moses, when they said to a prophet of theirs, “Appoint a king for us, so that we may fight in the path of God”? He said, “Might you, if fighting were prescribed for you, fail to fight?” They said, “Why should we not fight in the path of God, when we have been driven from our homes and our children?” Yet when fighting was prescribed for them, they turned away, except a few. God knows the wrongdoers.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The request and the anticipated retreat","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: have you not considered, Muḥammad, and taken a lesson from the story of a group of Israelites after Moses’ death? They sought to fight and said to a prophet of theirs called Shumwīl, or, according to another account, Shamʿūn: “Appoint a king for us to govern our affairs and to whom we may refer our decisions, for warfare cannot be conducted properly without a leader. We shall fight with him in God’s path.”<\/p><p>Their prophet said: “Might you, if fighting were prescribed for you, fail to fight?” Are you likely to turn away and flee if fighting is prescribed? That is, I anticipate that you will shrink from fighting if it is made obligatory. The underlying construction is: “You may shrink if it is made obligatory.” The interrogative <i>hal<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">هل<\/bdi>) is introduced before the verb of anticipation, questioning them about what he expects, to make them acknowledge and confirm their intention.<\/p><p>They replied: “What should prevent us from fighting in God’s path when the cause that calls for it exists?” Their enemy had gained power over them, driven them from their homes and taken their children captive. God Most High had subjected them to Jālūt and the Amalekites with him, who inhabited the coast of the Sea of the Romans between Egypt and Palestine. This happened when they disobeyed and shed blood. He laid Jerusalem waste, burned the Torah, took the Ark through which they had obtained victory, and captured their women and children. It is related that he captured four hundred and forty sons of their kings.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-199\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B189]<\/a><\/p><p>They therefore asked their prophet to appoint a king with whom they could fight. When fighting was prescribed and He provided them with a king to govern them, Ṭālūt, they grew afraid and turned away, except for a few: those who crossed the river with Ṭālūt. God knows the wrongdoers; He disgraces them and brings their counsels to ruin. We seek God’s protection from that.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: have you not considered, Muḥammad, and taken a lesson from the story of a group of Israelites after Moses’ death? They sought to fight and said to a prophet of theirs called Shumwīl, or, according to another account, Shamʿūn: “Appoint a king for us to govern our affairs and to whom we may refer our decisions, for warfare cannot be conducted properly without a leader. We shall fight with him in God’s path.” Their prophet said: “Might you, if fighting were prescribed for you, fail to fight?” Are you likely to turn away and flee if fighting is prescribed? That is, I anticipate that you will shrink from fighting if it is made obligatory. The underlying construction is: “You may shrink if it is made obligatory.” The interrogative hal ( هل ) is introduced before the verb of anticipation, questioning them about what he expects, to make them acknowledge and confirm their intention. They replied: “What should prevent us from fighting in God’s path when the cause that calls for it exists?” Their enemy had gained power over them, driven them from their homes and taken their children captive. God Most High had subjected them to Jālūt and the Amalekites with him, who inhabited the coast of the Sea of the Romans between Egypt and Palestine. This happened when they disobeyed and shed blood. He laid Jerusalem waste, burned the Torah, took the Ark through which they had obtained victory, and captured their women and children. It is related that he captured four hundred and forty sons of their kings. [B189] They therefore asked their prophet to appoint a king with whom they could fight. When fighting was prescribed and He provided them with a king to govern them, Ṭālūt, they grew afraid and turned away, except for a few: those who crossed the river with Ṭālūt. God knows the wrongdoers; He disgraces them and brings their counsels to ruin. We seek God’s protection from that."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The wish for a guide and the response when one appears","html":"<p>You see many people wishing they could find a shaykh of spiritual nurture. They say: “If we found him, we would struggle against our selves more than others.” Yet when he appears and becomes known for spiritual nurture, the person turns away and retreats, making excuses through denial and allegations of unfitness—except a few whom God singles out for His care. “God singles out for His mercy whom He wills”; “God is possessed of immense bounty.”<\/p><p>Glory to Him who has made the indication of His saints accessible only in the same manner as the indication of Himself, and who brings to them only those whom He wishes to bring to Himself.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"You see many people wishing they could find a shaykh of spiritual nurture. They say: “If we found him, we would struggle against our selves more than others.” Yet when he appears and becomes known for spiritual nurture, the person turns away and retreats, making excuses through denial and allegations of unfitness—except a few whom God singles out for His care. “God singles out for His mercy whom He wills”; “God is possessed of immense bounty.” Glory to Him who has made the indication of His saints accessible only in the same manner as the indication of Himself, and who brings to them only those whom He wishes to bring to Himself."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then identifies the king they had requested.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then identifies the king they had requested."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C273.1","page":273,"ar":"ويسمى الآن البحر المتوسط.","en":"It is now called the Mediterranean Sea.","anchor":"بحر الروم"},{"id":"C273.2","page":273,"ar":"الذراري: جمع ذرية، وهي النسل.","en":"Dharārī is the plural of dhurriyya, meaning offspring.","anchor":"وذراريهم"}],"english_characters":2612,"arabic_characters":2178}];
